writers, readers,\\n    25\\tsave/restore and invalidation. In implementation/repair evidence, explain changed\\n    26\\townership and migration of writers, or why existing ownership remains correct.\\n    27\\tFor prose-only work, explain which guidance responsibility changes and whether\\n    28\\texecutable ownership is unaffected.\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\tA missing consequential owner, incompatible writer, changed invariant or required\\n    31\\tscope expansion goes to the human with source evidence, consequence and options.\\n    32\\tContinue independent authorized work, preserving the unresolved decision. Routine\\n    33\\tchoices within an approved contract and already-authorized repairs can proceed.\\n    34\\tA reviewer cannot invent permission by recommending a new owner.\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t## Assign the whole ticket\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\tCapture the original starting baseline and exact current committed source; keep\\n    39\\tthe baseline fixed through repairs. Supply the full ticket diff from that baseline,\\n    40\\tapproved behavior/ownership contracts, role settings/guidance, coverage/check\\n    41\\trecords and relevant prior finding keys with append-only dispositions. Identify\\n    42\\tpending revalidation and human decisions explicitly. Keep separate fresh Spec and\\n    43\\tStandards assignments with independently configured settings. Each assesses the\\n    44\\tsource itself and records its original report before aggregation; sibling reports\\n    45\\tfrom the same review round are excluded from initial assessment context.\\n    46\\t\\n    47\\t- **Spec** checks acceptance requirements and approved behavior/ownership contracts,\\n    48\\t  including failures and compatibility. Trace collaborators where needed to decide\\n    49\\t  whether the promised behavior actually holds.\\n    50\\t- **Standards** follows affected state and responsibilities beyond changed hunks:\\n    51\\t  unchanged entry callers and consumers, every relevant writer, initialization,\\n    52\\t  persistence/restoration and invalidation. Inspect definitions and usages, then\\n    53\\t  follow the actual call/data flow; a changed-file list is a starting inventory.\\n    54\\t  Apply [codebase design](../../.agents/skills/matt-pocock/codebase-design/SKILL.md)\\n    55\\t  and the target's standards to explain who must coordinate each invariant.\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\tChoose scope by affected responsibilities and concrete risks; record omitted\\n    58\\tareas with reasons rather than claiming a whole-codebase review. A newly found\\n    59\\tout-of-scope breach stays visible as a concern/decision; it does not authorize a\\n    60\\tneighboring refactor. Existing authorized repair bounds, required checks,\\n    61\\tarchitecture checkpoint rules and milestone QA gates remain authoritative.\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\t## Report substantive reasoning\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\tUse the [report envelope](../templates/reviews/report.md). Each original report\\n    66\\tnames exact baseline/source and assignment/guidance identities, inspected files\\n    67\\tand flows (including unchanged collaborators), omitted scope with reasons and\\n    68\\tunresolved assumptions. Distinguish:\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\t1. **Substantive assessment:** connect the approved contract to actual operations,\\n    71\\t   writers, callers and restored state; explain whether responsibilities preserve\\n    72\\t   invariants. A clean report explains why the inspected design is acceptable.\\n    73\\t2. **Evidence audit:** report commands, hashes, test observations and their limits.\\n    74\\t   A matching hash proves identity; a passing test proves its observed behavior.\\n    75\\t   Neither establishes a design conclusion by itself.\\n    76\\t\\n    77\\tCheck each explicit guarantee against the accepted input values and reference\\n    78\\taliases in the supplied contract and implementation. A narrower input-domain\\n    79\\tassumption needs support from that contract or enforced validation; disclosing\\n    80\\tan assumption alone cannot establish a clean result when accepted inputs break\\n    81\\tthe guarantee. Record a concrete counterexample and classify its consequence\\n    82\\tunder the governing contract.\\n    83\\t\\n    84\\tFor each finding, cite exact code/contract locations, concrete evidence and\\n    85\\tconsequence, classify blocking versus optional, and give a proportionate remedy.\\n    86\\tA documented ownership breach can block even in unchanged code when it defeats\\n    87\\tthe ticket's approved outcome. A preference for classes, shorter functions or a\\n    88\\tparticular file layout alone is insufficient. A long cohesive function may be\\n    89\\tclean when its callers cross a small interface and it owns its decisions without\\n    90\\texternal coordination; explain that reasoning for the inspected flow.\\n    91\\t\\n    92\\tKeep the summary concise and link detail as needed. There is no universal short\\n    93\\treport cap: preserve all required scope, evidence and reasoning. Empty findings\\n    94\\tmean no findings within the recorded inspection, not universal design approval.\\n    95\\t\\n    96\\tFor cumulative milestone scope and start/post-implementation risk triggers, apply\\n    97\\t[architecture checkpoints](architecture-checkpoints.md). That contract determines\\n    98\\twhen broader work gates progression; this contract owns its substantive inspection.\\n    99\\t\\n   100\\t## Repair and handoff\\n   101\\t\\n   102\\tCarry the full review/report/local finding keys, original reports, disposition\\n   103\\thistory, approved contracts and exact rejected source to a fresh repair assignment.\\n   104\\tRecord its changed/unchanged ownership, claimed fixes and checks. Independently\\n   105\\tverify each claimed fix against the exact repaired revision and re-review the\\n   106\\twhole implementation from the original baseline using fresh separate axes.\\n   107\\tPreserve original reports and append verification/disposition events under the\\n   108\\t[record contract](review-records.md#immutable-reports-and-append-only-history).\\n   109\\tSource changes require relevant exact-source revalidation; historical clean\\n   110\\treports retain their original scope. Optional suggestions remain optional.\\n   111\\t\\n   112\\tA handoff links current ownership contracts/affected flows, assessment scope,\\n   113\\toriginal baseline/current source, unresolved full keys, missing reports, pending\\n   114\\trevalidation and human decisions with the responsible next action. State an\\n   115\\texplicit absence when none exists. Preserve active-run inputs, prior evidence,\\n   116\\tauthorized scope and milestone boundaries. Review completion and risk disposition\\n   117\\tremain separate from integration eligibility and human QA acceptance.\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c3/guidance/docs/agents/review-records.md\\n     1\\t# Review records\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tUse this development record contract when preparing review assignments, saving\\n     4\\treports, disposing findings or carrying review context into a repair/handoff.\\n     5\\tIt implements the local [review contract](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/spec.md#review-work-findings-and-decisions)\\n     6\\tunder the existing [tracker conventions](issue-tracker.md). It defines Markdown\\n     7\\trecords, not a runtime report schema, scheduler or additional review gate.\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\tBefore assigning source reviews or carrying ownership concerns into repairs or\\n    10\\thandoffs, apply [ownership and review scope](ownership-review.md). It owns the\\n    11\\tsubstantive cross-module inspection and reporting contract; these records own\\n    12\\tidentity, preservation and disposition mechanics.\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\t## Location and navigation\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\tKeep records with their feature: `.scratch/<feature>/reviews/` for work items,\\n    17\\thistories and decisions; `.scratch/<feature>/evidence/` for immutable reports,\\n    18\\tinstruction snapshots and check evidence. Link the review index from the feature\\n    19\\tindex and the originating ticket's history. Routine ticket Spec/Standards reports\\n    20\\tshare one ticket assessment; a cross-ticket assessment gets its own work item,\\n    21\\tlinked to every originating ticket and milestone. Use review IDs such as\\n    22\\t`review-ticket-16` or `review-M1-architecture-01`, independent of implementation\\n    23\\tticket IDs. These IDs neither consume ticket numbers nor select implementation\\n    24\\twork.\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\tStart with the [assessment](../templates/reviews/assessment.md),\\n    27\\t[report envelope](../templates/reviews/report.md),\\n    28\\t[finding history](../templates/reviews/history.md) and\\n    29\\t[human decision](../templates/reviews/decision.md) templates. Replace every\\n    30\\tplaceholder with a value or an explicit absence/reason; turn path placeholders\\n    31\\tinto relative Markdown links. Keep the original reviewer output verbatim as an\\n    32\\tattachment when adding an envelope would otherwise change it. Existing historical\\n    33\\treports retain their original formats and scope; link them without retroactive\\n    34\\tattestations. See the feature's [review index](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/reviews/README.md)\\n    35\\tand [isolated worked example](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/evidence/16-miniature/README.md).\\n    36\\t\\n    37\\tThe navigable chain is:\\n    38\\t\\n    39\\t`ticket/milestone \u2192 assessment \u2192 report/finding/evidence \u2192 history/decision \u2192 repair ticket/commit \u2192 independent verification \u2192 milestone assessment`.\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\tUse links in both directions where needed to return to the originating scope.\\n    42\\tIf a repair, decision or eventual milestone assessment does not exist, record\\n    43\\tthat absence and responsible next action rather than creating evidence of an\\n    44\\tassessment or approval that did not happen.\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\t## Assessment identity and lifecycle\\n    47\\t\\n    48\\tAn assessment identifies its purpose, originating ticket(s), approved plan and\\n    49\\tmilestone, fixed original baseline, exact assessed revision(s), reviewer axes,\\n    50\\tassignments/model/reasoning/context settings, and instruction identities. Identify\\n    51\\tinstructions by revision/path plus content digest or retained snapshot, including\\n    52\\tthe supplied spec/ticket and applicable guidance. Report unavailable settings as\\n    53\\tunavailable; intended settings alone are not proof of actual delivery.\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\tCapture inspected files/flows, omitted scope with reasons and unresolved\\n    56\\tassumptions, report/evidence links, and a current finding summary keyed as below.\\n    57\\tPreserve the original baseline through repairs. Each subsequent report identifies\\n    58\\tits own exact assessed revision and assignment; a clean re-review at a new source\\n    59\\tdoes not rewrite the earlier assessment.\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\tUse **Review lifecycle:** `planned`, `in progress`, or `completed`. Completed means\\n    62\\tthe defined assessment and its reports are finished. Keep unresolved findings,\\n    63\\tpending revalidation and integration/milestone eligibility in separate fields.\\n    64\\tThis lifecycle is independent of ticket triage `Status:`, execution state,\\n    65\\tverified ticket completion and human QA acceptance. An assessment can be completed\\n    66\\twith outstanding findings. A missing required report leaves it in progress.\\n    67\\t\\n    68\\t## Immutable reports and append-only history\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\tSave each original report/evidence snapshot at a unique path with report ID,\\n    71\\taxis/attempt/assignment identity, baseline, assessed source and content identity.\\n    72\\tRecord a digest in its index or capture manifest; avoid self-referential hashes.\\n    73\\tActor attribution and content identities provide provenance without new signing\\n    74\\tinfrastructure. Retain failed/rejected attempts as well as successful reports.\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\tA finding key is the tuple `(review ID, report ID, report-local finding ID)`.\\n    77\\tFor display, join these with `/` and choose IDs without `/`. For example,\\n    78\\t`review-ticket-16/spec-01/F1` differs from\\n    79\\t`review-ticket-16/standards-01/F1` and `review-ticket-16/spec-02/F1`.\\n    80\\tLink a later report's observation to the prior key when it supersedes or reopens\\n    81\\tthe same concern. Local IDs alone are never sufficient references.\\n    82\\t\\n    83\\tAppend numbered or uniquely identified events to the finding history. Each event\\n    84\\trecords the full key, disposition, actor/role, reason, evidence, affected revision,\\n    85\\tand links to the preceding/superseded observation, decision/amendment, repair\\n    86\\tticket, commit, independent verification and milestone assessment as applicable.\\n    87\\tUse explicit pending/not-applicable reasons for missing links. Preserve events\\n    88\\teven when mistaken: append a correction identifying what it supersedes. Reports,\\n    89\\tevidence and previous history events remain unchanged. The assessment's current\\n    90\\tsummary/index may be refreshed from that history; distinguish this mutable view\\n    91\\tfrom the authoritative observations.\\n    92\\t\\n    93\\t## Dispositions and authority\\n    94\\t\\n    95\\t| Disposition | Required support and next action |\\n    96\\t| --- | --- |\\n    97\\t| `open` | Original or reopened concern, source/evidence and impact; identify authorized repair or pending decision. |\\n    98\\t| `fixed` | Repair ticket/authorized repair, exact commit and independent verification of that exact revision. A worker's assertion or a passing mechanical check alone does not establish independent review of the concern. |\\n    99\\t| `deferred to linked ticket` | Follow-up ticket, reason, optional/blocking classification and authority. Optional suggestions may be deferred within approved scope. A blocker needs an explicit human decision and any required approved amendment. |\\n   100\\t| `accepted with reason` | Reason, affected source/risk and authority; consequential risk requires an explicit human review decision. |\\n   101\\t| `awaiting human decision` | Concrete question, consequence, proposed options, source and evidence; keep the concern unresolved while the decision is absent. |\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\tKeep severity/classification from the original report visible; a disposition\\n   104\\tdoes not silently reclassify a blocker. For a changed source, append a pending\\n   105\\trevalidation or reopened event and mark the current summary accordingly before\\n   106\\tusing an old fix as current evidence. Relevant independent revalidation must name\\n   107\\tthe new exact revision and concern; append its result, preserving the earlier fix.\\n   108\\tSource changes also require renewed applicable assessment/QA under the approved\\n   109\\tcontract. Unchanged or irrelevant evidence can be cited with its original scope;\\n   110\\tit cannot stand in for unperformed revalidation.\\n   111\\t\\n   112\\tAgents may carry out already-authorized repairs and record observations or\\n   113\\tproposals. Explicit human decisions own consequential risk acceptance, blocker\\n   114\\tdeferral, ownership-contract changes and scope expansion. Preserve the actual\\n   115\\thuman statement and attribution/context; link the approved amendment when the\\n   116\\tdecision changes an approved contract. Missing amendment means the blocker\\n   117\\tremains unresolved under that contract. A review disposition alone cannot\\n   118\\tauthorize integration, resume a stopped run or grant promotion. Assess eligibility\\n   119\\tunder the existing approved workflow; keep review decisions separate from\\n   120\\t[exact-candidate QA decisions](../adr/0002-human-qa-gates-feature-promotion.md).\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\t## Active runs and preserved evidence\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\tOutside active runs, author review records in the existing feature tracker and\\n   125\\tadd navigation links without altering historical reports, completed tickets'\\n   126\\toriginal requirements or frozen QA evidence. During a run, write new artifacts\\n   127\\tonly to harness-owned output outside approved inputs and worker-editable source.\\n   128\\tPreserve captured inputs and their identities. After the run, link those outputs\\n   129\\tfrom tracker history without rewriting active approved inputs or frozen evidence.\\n   130\\tIf the intended authoring files are active inputs, report that blocker and retain\\n   131\\tthe new record in the authorized output location until authoring is permitted.\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\tFor development architecture gating, apply\\n   134\\t[architecture checkpoints](architecture-checkpoints.md). Reviewer calibration and\\n   135\\truntime adoption remain tickets 19 and 33; development guidance is not runtime\\n   136\\tenforcement. These templates retain their evidence without claiming it exists.\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c3/guidance/docs/agents/architecture-checkpoints.md\\n     1\\t# Development architecture checkpoints\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tUse this contract when implementing tickets, preparing integration, or writing a\\n     4\\tmilestone QA handoff. It implements the approved\\n     5\\t[cumulative checkpoint contract](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/spec.md#cumulative-architecture-checkpoints)\\n     6\\tthrough repo-owned guidance and tracker records. Automated runtime enforcement\\n     7\\tbelongs to ticket 33. Apply [ownership and review scope](ownership-review.md) for\\n     8\\tsubstantive assessment and [review records](review-records.md) for identities,\\n     9\\timmutable reports, finding history and human authority.\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\t## Decide when review is required\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\tAt **ticket start and after implementation**, record yes/no with source evidence\\n    14\\tfor each trigger: a new lifecycle, persistence/restoration path, shared mutable\\n    15\\tstate, or additional writer of existing state. Include guidance state when a\\n    16\\tprose change introduces one of these responsibilities. Explain unchanged owners\\n    17\\tand affected entry callers, writers, consumers, restore and invalidation paths.\\n    18\\tA trigger requires a broader assessment of that affected workflow **before ticket\\n    19\\tintegration**. Missing or incomplete require\u2026194 tokens truncated\u202631\\t## Pin the cumulative work\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\tUse the [assessment template](../templates/reviews/assessment.md). Record:\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t- Purpose and originating milestone/tickets; the trigger assessment at start and\\n    36\\t  after implementation, and whether the checkpoint gates integration or QA.\\n    37\\t- **Cumulative baseline:** exact last human-accepted milestone source, linked to\\n    38\\t  its actual QA decision. For the first milestone, use the feature starting\\n    39\\t  revision with Git/planning provenance. This is distinct from a ticket's newly\\n    40\\t  captured fixed review baseline. Never infer acceptance from clean reviews or\\n    41\\t  choose the repository's first commit without feature provenance. If the\\n    42\\t  starting revision or acceptance evidence is missing/ambiguous, expose that gap\\n    43\\t  and hold readiness while the responsible workflow establishes it.\\n    44\\t- Exact assessed source commit plus source/instruction identities; complete file\\n    45\\t  and workflow inventory, with omitted scope and reasons. For this repository's\\n    46\\t  small package, inspect **all `ticket_harness` Python files**, including unchanged\\n    47\\t  collaborators. Larger targets inventory the complete affected workflow and its\\n    48\\t  collaborators. A list of files alone does not establish inspection.\\n    49\\t- All prior unresolved full finding keys, original reports, disposition/decision/\\n    50\\t  amendment/verification links, and pending exact-source revalidation. Record an\\n    51\\t  explicit absence when there are none; retain optional/deferred observations.\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\tUse a **fresh High Standards assignment**, with the approved model, role guidance\\n    54\\tand active-context target. Keep it distinct from routine ticket attempts and\\n    55\\tpreserve their independent Spec/Standards axes. Report unavailable settings.\\n    56\\tArchitecture review guidance can inform the assessment; an HTML report is not\\n    57\\trequired for a checkpoint. The original report must explain actual owners,\\n    58\\toperations, unchanged writers/consumers and restored/invalidation behavior. Trace\\n    59\\tthe concrete edits for a plausible change (for example another review stage or\\n    60\\trepair-policy adjustment): which owner, callers, saved representation, restore,\\n    61\\tinvalidation and checks would change, and why. Hashes and passing tests support\\n    62\\tidentity/behavior, not the design conclusion.\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\tAn existing audit can satisfy this work only when its **exact source, complete\\n    65\\trequired coverage and retained dispositions** match the present checkpoint. Link\\n    66\\tthat proof and the original audit. A changed source requires renewed relevant\\n    67\\treview; record scope and why any unchanged evidence remains applicable. Until\\n    68\\tthat review names the current source, prior readiness is stale. Do not edit old\\n    69\\treports to make them appear current.\\n    70\\t\\n    71\\t## Findings and progression\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\tApply the [disposition and authority rules](review-records.md#dispositions-and-authority):\\n    74\\tfixed needs independent verification of the exact repair revision; deferral needs\\n    75\\ta linked ticket and reason; consequential risk acceptance, blocker deferral,\\n    76\\tchanged ownership or scope requires an actual human decision and applicable\\n    77\\tapproved amendment. Keep awaiting decisions and missing amendments unresolved.\\n    78\\tPreserve original classification and append history; never obtain authority by\\n    79\\trelabeling a blocker. Optional suggestions remain optional and cause no automatic\\n    80\\trepair or approval request.\\n    81\\t\\n    82\\tRecord four separate observations, supported by source-specific evidence:\\n    83\\t\\n    84\\t| Observation | Meaning and next action |\\n    85\\t| --- | --- |\\n    86\\t| Assessment completed | Required reports and inspection finished; findings can remain open. Missing reports mean in progress. |\\n    87\\t| Findings resolved/dispositioned | Exact fixes independently verified or valid authority and linked follow-up retained. Open in-scope blockers still prevent affected integration/readiness. |\\n    88\\t| Candidate ready for human QA | Required membership, current architecture work, finding gates and all existing exact-candidate checks are satisfied. Only the authorized QA workflow prepares the actual request. |\\n    89\\t| Milestone accepted | Actual human QA decision accepts the exact candidate/inputs. Only this permits the subsequent milestone/promotion under the approved workflow. |\\n    90\\t\\n    91\\tA clean checkpoint progresses autonomously within approved scope; it supplies no\\n    92\\thuman acceptance. Authorized in-scope blockers use existing bounded repairs.\\n    93\\tA cumulative blocker outside those bounds or a consequential undecided contract\\n    94\\tpreserves work and produces an incomplete human-decision handoff; independent\\n    95\\tunaffected work may continue only under the existing eligibility rules. A review\\n    96\\tdecision cannot resume a stopped run or accept a candidate.\\n    97\\t\\n    98\\t## Tracker and handoff\\n    99\\t\\n   100\\tLink the assessment from every originating ticket, the milestone and feature\\n   101\\treview index. Carry baseline/current source, complete scope, lifecycle, missing\\n   102\\treports, unresolved full keys, deferred ticket links, human decisions/amendments,\\n   103\\tstale evidence and the next responsible action into implementation and QA/waiting\\n   104\\thandoffs. Distinguish an absent checkpoint from a clean one. Refresh the current\\n   105\\tsummary while preserving reports and append-only history.\\n   106\\t\\n   107\\tPreserve approved active-run inputs and frozen QA snapshots. During a run, new\\n   108\\tartifacts belong only in authorized harness output, with authoring links added\\n   109\\tlater. If the requested authoring path is an active input, report that exact\\n   110\\tblocker. Changed assessed source or frozen evidence invalidates relevant readiness;\\n   111\\tnew proposals may remain outside frozen evidence pending renewed review/request.\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\tThe [ticket 18 walkthrough](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/evidence/18-walkthrough/README.md)\\n   114\\tretains finite isolated observations. Its example human statements are synthetic;\\n   115\\tits reviews do not assess or accept the real development milestone.\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c3/guidance/docs/templates/reviews/report.md\\n     1\\t# Review report envelope: {{review ID}} / {{report ID}}\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tTemplate: [record contract](../../agents/review-records.md). Preserve original\\n     4\\treviewer output verbatim at a unique evidence path; store this envelope alongside\\n     5\\tit if the report lacks metadata. Record envelope/output digests in the assessment\\n     6\\tor manifest after capture, outside the hashed file itself.\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\t| Field | Value |\\n     9\\t| --- | --- |\\n    10\\t| Assessment | {{review ID and work item link}} |\\n    11\\t| Report identity | {{report ID, axis, attempt, assignment and actor identity}} |\\n    12\\t| Actual settings | {{model, reasoning, context target/observed setting or unavailable}} |\\n    13\\t| Original baseline | {{full source identity}} |\\n    14\\t| Assessed source | {{exact full revision or retained snapshot identity}} |\\n    15\\t| Supplied instructions | {{paths, revision and digests/snapshot manifest; missing delivery evidence explicit}} |\\n    16\\t| Original output/evidence | {{immutable relative links and capture manifest}} |\\n    17\\t| Inspected scope | {{files/flows}} |\\n    18\\t| Omitted scope | {{scope and reasons; unresolved assumptions}} |\\n    19\\t| Prior observations | {{superseded/reopened full keys and links or none}} |\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\t## Substantive assessment\\n    22\\t\\n    23\\t{{Approved behavior/ownership contract; traced callers, writers, persistence/restore\\n    24\\tand invalidation; reasoning about invariant preservation, including a justified\\n    25\\tclean conclusion when appropriate. Apply the shared\\n    26\\t[scope contract](../../agents/ownership-review.md#report-substantive-reasoning).}}\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\t## Evidence audit\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\t{{Commands/observations and identity checks, results and limits; distinguish these\\n    31\\tfrom the substantive design conclusion.}}\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t## Findings\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\tUse a heading per report-local ID. Preserve the reviewer's classification,\\n    36\\tconcern, source location/evidence, consequence and proposed remedy. Dispositions\\n    37\\tbelong in the separate append-only history, linked by the full compound key.\\n    38\\t\\n    39\\t### {{local ID}}\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\t{{Classification; concern; exact source/evidence; consequence; remedy; full key}}\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\t## Assessment result\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\t{{Assessment complete/incomplete; unresolved assumptions; original result.\\n    46\\tThis snapshot is not a mutable current-disposition summary.}}\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c3/guidance/GLOSSARY.md\\n     1\\t# Ticket Execution\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tThe workflow for carrying approved software work through implementation, verification, and local integration.\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t## Language\\n     6\\t\\n     7\\t**Spec**:\\n     8\\tThe agreed description of intended behavior, constraints, and acceptance requirements for a body of work.\\n     9\\t_Avoid_: Plan (when referring to required outcomes)\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\t**Ticket**:\\n    12\\tA bounded piece of work under an approved spec, with its own acceptance requirements and declared dependencies on other tickets.\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\t**Approved plan**:\\n    15\\tThe human-accepted version of a spec, its tickets, milestone membership, and acceptance conditions.\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t**Milestone**:\\n    18\\tA planned group of ticket outcomes with human acceptance conditions that gates further feature work and promotion.\\n    19\\t\\n    20\\t**QA decision**:\\n    21\\tThe human's recorded acceptance or request for corrections for a specific milestone candidate.\\n    22\\t\\n    23\\t**Skill selection**:\\n    24\\tThe skills approved for a role's work, combining its required role skills, project defaults, and any applicable ticket additions.\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\t**Run**:\\n    27\\tAn execution of the harness against an approved spec and selected tickets, together with its recorded outcomes.\\n    28\\t\\n    29\\t**Implementation attempt**:\\n    30\\tA worker assignment to produce a ticket implementation and submit its result. Development and testing iterations within that assignment belong to the same attempt.\\n    31\\t\\n    32\\t**Repair attempt**:\\n    33\\tAn implementation attempt made in response to a rejected submission, using the current changes and the reported failures as its starting point.\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t**Blocking finding**:\\n    36\\tA reviewer-reported issue that must be resolved before a ticket may be integrated. It can trigger repair without immediately putting the ticket into the blocked state.\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t**Optional suggestion**:\\n    39\\tA reviewer-reported improvement that does not prevent integration or require an automatic repair attempt.\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\t**Harness**:\\n    42\\tThe system responsible for coordinating ticket execution, verification, bounded repairs, and integration for approved work.\\n    43\\t_Avoid_: Coordinator agent, dispatcher agent\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\t**Target repository**:\\n    46\\tThe Git repository in which the harness carries out approved work. Its project commands, standards, and constraints govern that work.\\n    47\\t_Avoid_: Client repository\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t**Integration branch**:\\n    50\\tThe local destination branch into which verified changes are combined. Its role may be a feature branch or a development branch; integration does not imply permission to publish.\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\t**Feature branch**:\\n    53\\tThe integration branch that accumulates verified ticket changes for one approved feature before milestone acceptance.\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\t**Development branch**:\\n    56\\tThe shared integration branch that receives accepted milestone candidates from feature branches, conventionally named `dev`.\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\t**Promotion**:\\n    59\\tThe advancement of the development branch to a verified milestone candidate accepted by the human.\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t**Candidate merge**:\\n    62\\tThe proposed combination of source changes and a destination branch, evaluated before that destination advances. A ticket candidate targets the feature branch; a milestone candidate targets the development branch.\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\t**Review work item**:\\n    65\\tAn identified assessment of a defined source revision and scope, linked to its reports and outstanding findings. Completing the assessment does not itself resolve its findings or accept a milestone.\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\t**Review report**:\\n    68\\tThe preserved result of one reviewer assignment against an identified source and scope, including inspected coverage, findings and unresolved assumptions.\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\t**Finding disposition**:\\n    71\\tThe recorded treatment of a review finding, with its reason, authority and supporting evidence. Its history connects the original concern to a verified fix, linked deferral or explicit human decision.\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t**Review decision**:\\n    74\\tA human's recorded choice about consequential review risk, scope, ownership or deferral. It is separate from a QA decision accepting an exact milestone candidate.\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\t**Architecture checkpoint**:\\n    77\\tA required cumulative assessment of responsibility and invariant ownership across an affected workflow before specified integration or milestone-readiness progression.\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c3/guidance/docs/agents/testing.md\\n     1\\t# Testing policy\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tThis policy governs development of this repository's harness. The reusable\\n     4\\t[test-strategy skill](../../.agents/skills/custom/test-strategy/SKILL.md) guides\\n     5\\tcoverage selection and suite audits; [TDD](../../.agents/skills/matt-pocock/tdd/SKILL.md)\\n     6\\tguides implementation. Read [Python environment guidance](python-environment.md)\\n     7\\tbefore Python or checks. The [harness guide](../ticket-harness.md#python-and-checks)\\n     8\\tdocuments the check commands and tool setup.\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t## Coverage selection\\n    11\\t\\n    12\\tMap acceptance requirements to existing tests before adding cases. Extend or\\n    13\\treuse those tests where they already exercise the required behavior. For a new\\n    14\\tcase, identify the distinct failure it detects. Keep the short map in the ticket's\\n    15\\texisting evidence or implementation report.\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\tThe approved harness seam is the public CLI with scripted external processes,\\n    18\\treal temporary Git repositories, and observable Git/report outcomes. Preserve that\\n    19\\tagreement. A cheaper public module seam is a design proposal to resolve with the\\n    20\\thuman, not permission to replace behavioral coverage with internal mocks.\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\tCover meaningful role, attempt, and failure-policy differences and interactions.\\n    23\\tUse representative equivalent inputs rather than automatically multiplying every\\n    24\\tdimension. Explicit ticket scenarios and demonstrated regressions remain required.\\n    25\\tSeveral acceptance criteria may share one scenario; separate test names do not\\n    26\\tnecessarily imply separate coverage. Preserve isolated writable repositories even\\n    27\\twhen sharing fixture builders or setup helpers.\\n    28\\t\\n    29\\t## Which checks to run\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\t| Change or stage | Verification |\\n    32\\t| --- | --- |\\n    33\\t| Development iteration | Focused tests for the behavior and affected callers/fixtures, plus relevant static checks. Expand when shared impact is uncertain. |\\n    34\\t| Implementation ticket checkpoint | The full test suite and required strict type, lint, format, and whitespace checks once the final implementation is ready. Explicit ticket requirements also apply. |\\n    35\\t| Further code, test, configuration, or dependency changes | Rerun affected checks and renew the full-suite completion result for the final implementation. |\\n    36\\t| Packaging or entry-point changes | Include wheel/install and installed CLI checks. Include other installed scenarios when explicitly required or when they resolve a concrete uncertainty. |\\n    37\\t| Standalone documentation or skill changes | Validate the changed documents/skills, links, routing, and relevant workflow decisions. Runtime tests are warranted when executable examples or runtime behavior are affected. |\\n    38\\t| Bookkeeping or handoff after verified implementation | Check the changed records, links, and consistency. Preserve the implementation's recorded check provenance; prose-only bookkeeping does not require replaying its suite. |\\n    39\\t\\n    40\\tAn implementation ticket's full-suite gate remains required even when focused tests\\n    41\\tpass. This policy does not establish selective testing as a substitute for that\\n    42\\tgate, or override a ticket's explicit checks. Missing or failing required checks\\n    43\\tleave verification incomplete.\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\tFor focused runs, select existing files or cases by the behavior under change.\\n    46\\tFor example, after reading the case to confirm its relevance:\\n    47\\t\\n    48\\t```bash\\n    49\\tpython -m pytest tests/test_repairs.py -q -k failed_check\\n    50\\t```\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\tUse timing output during the required full run when investigating suite cost:\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t```bash\\n    55\\tpython -m pytest -q --durations=20\\n    56\\t```\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\tInspect prerequisite completion records and their relationship to the current\\n    59\\timplementation. Independently rerun a prerequisite scenario when there is a\\n    60\\tspecific compatibility uncertainty; the final full suite already includes the\\n    61\\tprerequisite regression tests. Fresh reviewers may reproduce selected behavior\\n    62\\twithout each repeating the full matrix.\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\t## Evidence and review\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\tArrange required evidence capture before running checks. Record the command,\\n    67\\texit result, actual source/test revision (and relevant uncommitted changes), and\\n    68\\tretained logs/artifact locations. Existing test assertions and their results can\\n    69\\tsupport several criteria. Preserve required CLI/Git evidence from that execution\\n    70\\twhere available; extra saved JSON is not automatically a reason for a second\\n    71\\tcomplete fixture run. If a required observation was not retained, collect the\\n    72\\tmissing evidence with a scoped run. Report remaining gaps rather than inferring\\n    73\\tunobserved results.\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tSupply the coverage map and this policy to the existing Spec and Standards\\n    76\\treviewers when tests change. Test value, unnecessary combinations, repeated setup,\\n    77\\tfixture ownership, isolation, and reliability belong in the existing review.\\n    78\\tOptional maintenance suggestions remain optional.\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\tFor a requested or measured suite audit, use the test-strategy skill. Propose\\n    81\\tconsolidations with an explicit mapping to the surviving regression coverage and\\n    82\\tbefore/after check evidence. Runtime and maintenance cost are useful measures;\\n    83\\treducing the test count alone is not a goal.\\n    84\\t\\n    85\\t## Harness verification remains separate\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\tThese choices concern how developers verify changes to the harness. During a\\n    88\\tharness run, all configured baseline, lightweight, review, and exact-candidate\\n    89\\tgates still apply. Source changes invalidate approvals under the existing rules.\\n    90\\tA development test selection or a prior passing tree cannot authorize skipping\\n    91\\ta required gate or reusing approval for a different candidate revision.\\n\"}"
