pment-review-19-c4/cases/h8/attempt.py\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c4/REVIEW.md\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c4/cases/h8/CONTRACT.md\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c4/cases/e4/worker.py\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c4/cases/e4/attempt.py\\n/tmp/development-review-19-c4/cases/e4/CONTRACT.md\\n# Ownership and review scope\\n\\nUse this shared development contract when planning consequential mutable state,\\nimplementing or repairing its operations, assigning Spec/Standards review, or\\nwriting the next-agent handoff. It supplements [review records](review-records.md)\\nand the approved spec; it defines guidance, not a runtime schema or new gate.\\nApply [domain guidance](domain.md), accepted ADRs and the target's own contracts.\\n\\n## Planning and implementation\\n\\nRecord each consequential state contract in the spec's implementation decisions\\n(or link its existing authoritative contract). Identify:\\n\\n| Decision | Required content |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| State and owner | The state, responsible module and invariants it owns. |\\n| Permitted writers | All roles/modules allowed to change it, including initialization and recovery. |\\n| Operations | The operations through which writers preserve invariants and ordering. |\\n| Persistence and restoration | Saved representation, reconstruction rules, shared identity/reference requirements and invalidation. State explicitly when no durable representation exists. |\\n| Failures | Observable rejection/failure behavior, partial-transition handling and who records the outcome. |\\n\\nDescribe responsibilities and operations; internal class, method and file choices\\nremain worker decisions. Before implementation, locate the approved contract and\\nrecord the affected flow: entry callers, owner operations, other writers, readers,\\nsave/restore and invalidation. In implementation/repair evidence, explain changed\\nownership and migration of writers, or why existing ownership remains correct.\\nFor prose-only work, explain which guidance responsibility changes and whether\\nexecutable ownership is unaffected.\\n\\nA missing consequential owner, incompatible writer, changed invariant or required\\nscope expansion goes to the human with source evidence, consequence and options.\\nContinue independent authorized work, preserving the unresolved decision. Routine\\nchoices within an approved contract and already-authorized repairs can proceed.\\nA reviewer cannot invent permission by recommending a new owner.\\n\\n## Assign the whole ticket\\n\\nCapture the original starting baseline and exact current committed source; keep\\nthe baseline fixed through repairs. Supply the full ticket diff from that baseline,\\napproved behavior/ownership contracts, role settings/guidance, coverage/check\\nrecords and relevant prior finding keys with append-only dispositions. Identify\\npending revalidation and human decisions explicitly. Keep separate fresh Spec and\\nStandards assignments with independently configured settings. Each assesses the\\nsource itself and records its original report before aggregation; sibling reports\\nfrom the same review round are excluded from initial assessment context.\\n\\n- **Spec** checks acceptance requirements and approved behavior/ownership contracts,\\n  including failures and compatibility. Trace collaborators where needed to decide\\n  whether the promised behavior actually holds.\\n- **Standards** follows affected state and responsibilities beyond changed hunks:\\n  unchanged entry callers and consumers, every relevant writer, initialization,\\n  persistence/restoration and invalidation. Inspect definitions and usages, then\\n  follow the actual call/data flow; a changed-file list is a starting inventory.\\n  Apply [codebase design](../../.agents/skills/matt-pocock/codebase-design/SKILL.md)\\n  and the target's standards to explain who must coordinate each invariant.\\n\\nChoose scope by affected responsibilities and concrete risks; record omitted\\nareas with reasons rather than claiming a whole-codebase review. A newly found\\nout-of-scope breach stays visible as a concern/decision; it does not authorize a\\nneighboring refactor. Existing authorized repair bounds, required checks,\\narchitecture checkpoint rules and milestone QA gates remain authoritative.\\n\\n## Report substantive reasoning\\n\\nUse the [report envelope](../templates/reviews/report.md). Each original report\\nnames exact baseline/source and assignment/guidance identities, inspected files\\nand flows (including unchanged collaborators), omitted scope with reasons and\\nunresolved assumptions. Distinguish:\\n\\n1. **Substantive assessment:** connect the approved contract to actual operations,\\n   writers, callers and restored state; explain whether responsibilities preserve\\n   invariants. A clean report explains why the inspected design is acceptable.\\n2. **Evidence audit:** report commands, hashes, test observations and their limits.\\n   A matching hash proves identity; a passing test proves its observed behavior.\\n   Neither establishes a design conclusion by itself.\\n\\nCheck each explicit guarantee against the accepted input values and reference\\naliases in the supplied contract and implementation. A narrower input-domain\\nassumption needs support from that contract or enforced validation; disclosing\\nan assumption alone cannot establish a clean result when accepted inputs break\\nthe guarantee. Record a concrete counterexample and classify its consequence\\nunder the governing contract.\\n\\nFor each finding, cite exact code/contract locations, concrete evidence and\\nconsequence, classify blocking versus optional, and give a proportionate remedy.\\nA documented ownership breach can block even in unchanged code when it defeats\\nthe ticket's approved outcome. A preference for classes, shorter functions or a\\nparticular file layout alone is insufficient. A long cohesive function may be\\nclean when its callers cross a small interface and it owns its decisions without\\nexternal coordination; explain that reasoning for the inspected flow.\\n\\nKeep the summary concise and link detail as needed. There is no universal short\\nreport cap: preserve all required scope, evidence and reasoning. Empty findings\\nmean no findings within the recorded inspection, not universal design approval.\\n\\nFor cumulative milestone scope and start/post-implementation risk triggers, apply\\n[architecture checkpoints](architecture-checkpoints.md). That contract determines\\nwhen broader work gates progression; this contract owns its substantive inspection.\\n\\n## Repair and handoff\\n\\nCarry the full review/report/local finding keys, original reports, disposition\\nhistory, approved contracts and exact rejected source to a fresh repair assignment.\\nRecord its changed/unchanged ownership, claimed fixes and checks. Independently\\nverify each claimed fix against the exact repaired revision and re-review the\\nwhole implementation from the original baseline using fresh separate axes.\\nPreserve original reports and append verification/disposition events under the\\n[record contract](review-records.md#immutable-reports-and-append-only-history).\\nSource changes require relevant exact-source revalidation; historical clean\\nreports retain their original scope. Optional suggestions remain optional.\\n\\nA handoff links current ownership contracts/affected flows, assessment scope,\\noriginal baseline/current source, unresolved full keys, missing reports, pending\\nrevalidation and human decisions with the responsible next action. State an\\nexplicit absence when none exists. Preserve active-run inputs, prior evidence,\\nauthorized scope and milestone boundaries. Review completion and risk disposition\\nremain separate from integration eligibility and human QA acceptance.\\n# Review records\\n\\nUse this development record contract when preparing review assignments, saving\\nreports, disposing findings or carrying review context into a repair/handoff.\\nIt implements the local [review contract](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/spec.md#review-work-findings-and-decisions)\\nunder the existing [tracker conventions](issue-tracker.md). It defines Markdown\\nrecords, not a runtime report schema, scheduler or additional review gate.\\n\\nBefore assigning source reviews or carrying ownership concerns into repairs or\\nhandoffs, apply [ownership and review scope](ownership-review.md). It owns the\\nsubstantive cross-module inspection and reporting contract; these records own\\nidentity, preservation and disposition mechanics.\\n\\n## Location and navigation\\n\\nKeep records with their feature: `.scratch/<feature>/reviews/` for work items,\\nhistories and decisions; `.scratch/<feature>/evidence/` for immutable reports,\\ninstruction snapshots and check evidence. Link the review index from the feature\\nindex and the originating ticket's history. Routine ticket Spec/Standards reports\\nshare one ticket assessment; a cross-ticket assessment gets its own work item,\\nlinked to every originating ticket and milestone. Use review IDs such as\\n`review-ticket-16` or `review-M1-architecture-01`, independent of implementation\\nticket IDs. These IDs neither consume ticket numbers nor select implementation\\nwork.\\n\\nStart with the [assessment](../templates/reviews/assessment.md),\\n[report envelope](../templates/reviews/report.md),\\n[finding history](../templates/reviews/history.md) and\\n[human decision](../templates/reviews/decision.md) templates. Replace every\\nplaceholder with a value or an explicit absence/reason; turn path placeholders\\ninto relative Markdown links. Keep the original reviewer output verbatim as an\\nattachment when adding an envelope would otherwise change it. Existing historical\\nreports retain their original formats and scope; link them without retroactive\\nattestations. See the feature's [review index](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/reviews/README.md)\\nand [isolated worked example](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/evidence/16-miniature/README.md).\\n\\nThe navigable chain is:\\n\\n`ticket/milestone \u2192 assessment \u2192 report/finding/evidence \u2192 history/decision \u2192 repair ticket/commit \u2192 independent verification \u2192 milestone assessment`.\\n\\nUse links in both directions where needed to return to the originating scope.\\nIf a repair, decision or eventual milestone assessment does not exist, record\\nthat absence and responsible next action rather than creating evidence of an\\nassessment or approval that did not happen.\\n\\n## Assessment identity and lifecycle\\n\\nAn assessment identifies its purpose, originating ticket(s), approved plan and\\nmilestone, fixed original baseline, exact assessed revision(s), reviewer axes,\\nassignments/model/reasoning/context settings, and instruction identities. Identify\\ninstructions by revision/path plus content digest or retained snapshot, including\\nthe supplied spec/ticket and applicable guidance. Report unavailable settings as\\nunavailable; intended settings alone are not proof of actual delivery.\\n\\nCapture inspected files/flows, omitted scope with reasons and unresolved\\nassumptions, report/evidence links, and a current finding summary keyed as below.\\nPreserve the original baseline through repairs. Each subsequent report identifies\\nits own exact assessed revision and assignment; a clean re-review at a new source\\ndoes not rewrite the earlier assessment.\\n\\nUse **Review lifecycle:** `planned`, `in progress`, or `completed`. Completed means\\nthe defined assessment and its reports are finished. Keep unresolved findings,\\npending revalidation and integration/milestone eligibility in separate fields.\\nThis lifecycle is independent of ticket triage `Status:`, execution state,\\nverified ticket completion and human QA acceptance. An assessment can be completed\\nwith outstanding findings. A missing required report leaves it in progress.\\n\\n## Immutable reports and append-only history\\n\\nSave each original report/evidence snapshot at a unique path with report ID,\\naxis/attempt/assignment identity, baseline, assessed source and content identity.\\nRecord a digest in its index or capture manifest; avoid self-referential hashes.\\nActor attribution and content identities provide provenance without new signing\\ninfrastructure. Retain failed/rejected attempts as well as successful reports.\\n\\nA finding key is the tuple `(review ID, report ID, report-local finding ID)`.\\nFor display, join these with `/` and choose IDs without `/`. For example,\\n`review-ticket-16/spec-01/F1` differs from\\n`review-ticket-16/standards-01/F1` and `review-ticket-16/spec-02/F1`.\\nLink a later report's observation to the prior key when it supersedes or reopens\\nthe same concern. Local IDs alone are never sufficient references.\\n\\nAppend numbered or uniquely identified events to the finding history. Each event\\nrecords the full key, disposition, actor/role, reason, evidence, affected revision,\\nand links to the preceding/superseded observation, decision/amendment, repair\\nticket, commit, independent verification and milestone assessment as applicable.\\nUse explicit pending/not-applicable reasons for missing links. Preserve events\\neven when mistaken: append a correction identifying what it supersedes. Reports,\\nevidence and previous history events remain unchanged. The assessment's current\\nsummary/index may be refreshed from that history; distinguish this mutable view\\nfrom the authoritative observations.\\n\\n## Dispositions and authority\\n\\n| Disposition | Required support and next action |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `open` | Original or reopened concern, source/evidence and impact; identify authorized repair or pending decision. |\\n| `fixed` | Repair ticket/authorized repair, exact commit and independent verification of that exact revision. A worker's assertion or a passing mechanical check alone does not establish independent review of the concern. |\\n| `deferred to linked ticket` | Follow-up ticket, reason, optional/blocking classification and authority. Optional suggestions may be deferred within approved scope. A blocker needs an explicit human decision and any required approved amendment. |\\n| `accepted with reason` | Reason, affected source/risk and authority; consequential risk requires an explicit human review decision. |\\n| `awaiting human decision` | Concrete question, consequence, proposed options, source and evidence; keep the concern unresolved while the decision is absent. |\\n\\nKeep severity/classification from the original report visible; a disposition\\ndoes not silently reclassify a blocker. For a changed source, append a pending\\nrevalidation or reopened event and mark the current summary accordingly before\\nusing an old fix as current evidence. Relevant independent revalidation must name\\nthe new exact revision and concern; append its result, preserving the earlier fix.\\nSource changes also require renewed applicable assessment/QA under the approved\\ncontract. Unchanged or irrelevant evidence can be cited with its original scope;\\nit cannot stand in for unperformed revalidation.\\n\\nAgents may carry out already-authorized repairs and record observations or\\nproposals. Explicit human decisions own consequential risk acceptance, blocker\\ndeferral, ownership-contract changes and scope expansion. Preserve the actual\\nhuman statement and attribution/context; link the approved amendment when the\\ndecision changes an approved contract. Missing amendment means the blocker\\nremains unresolved under that contract. A review disposition alone cannot\\nauthorize integration, resume a stopped run or grant promotion. Assess eligibility\\nunder the existing approved workflow; keep review decisions separate from\\n[exact-candidate QA decisions](../adr/0002-human-qa-gates-feature-promotion.md).\\n\\n## Active runs and preserved evidence\\n\\nOutside active runs, author review records in the existing feature tracker and\\nadd navigation links without altering historical reports, completed tickets'\\noriginal requirements or frozen QA evidence. During a run, write new artifacts\\nonly to harness-owned output outside approved inputs and worker-editable source.\\nPreserve captured inputs and their identities. After the run, link those outputs\\nfrom tracker history without rewriting active approved inputs or frozen evidence.\\nIf the intended authoring files are active inputs, report that blocker and retain\\nthe new record in the authorized output location until authoring is permitted.\\n\\nFor development architecture gating, apply\\n[architecture checkpoints](architecture-checkpoints.md). Reviewer calibration and\\nruntime adoption remain tickets 19 and 33; development guidance is not runtime\\nenforcement. These templates retain their evidence without claiming it exists.\\n# Development architecture checkpoints\\n\\nUse this contract when implementing tickets, preparing integration, or writing a\\nmilestone QA handoff. It implements the approved\\n[cumulative checkpoint contract](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/spec.md#cumulative-architecture-checkpoints)\\nthrough repo-owned guidance and tracker records. Automated runtime enforcement\\nbelongs to ticket 33. Apply [ownership and review scope](ownership-review.md) for\\nsubstantive assessment and [review records](review-records.md) for identities,\\nimmutable reports, finding history and human authority.\\n\\n## Decide when review is required\\n\\nAt **ticket start and after implementation**, record yes/no with source evidence\\nfor each trigger: a new lifecycle, persistence/restoration path, shared mutable\\nstate, or additional writer of existing state. Include guidance state when a\\nprose change introduces one of these responsibilities. Explain unchanged owners\\nand affected entry callers, writers, consumers, restore and invalidation paths.\\nA trigger requires a broader assessment of that affected workflow **before ticket\\nintegration**. Missing or incomplete required work remains an explicit blocker;\\nordinary ticket Spec/Standards reviews still run independently. A no-trigger\\nresult needs no third unconditional reviewer. Reassess after repairs when their\\nscope changes the trigger result.\\n\\nBefore **each required development milestone QA request**, create a cumulative\\nreview work item in the feature's review index. Do this before presenting a\\ncandidate as ready or freezing its assessment evidence. Planning may create the\\nitem early with lifecycle `planned`; missing reports stay visible. Required\\nmembership completion alone produces a preparation/waiting handoff until the\\ncheckpoint and other candidate gates are satisfied.\\n\\n## Pin the cumulative work\\n\\nUse the [assessment template](../templates/reviews/assessment.md). Record:\\n\\n- Purpose and originating milestone/tickets; the trigger assessment at start and\\n  after implementation, and whether the checkpoint gates integration or QA.\\n- **Cumulative baseline:** exact last human-accepted milestone source, linked to\\n  its actual QA decision. For the first milestone, use the feature starting\\n  revision with Git/planning provenance. This is distinct from a ticket's newly\\n  captured fixed review baseline. Never infer acceptance from clean reviews or\\n  choose the repository's first commit without feature provenance. If the\\n  starting revision or acceptance evidence is missing/ambiguous, expose that gap\\n  and hold readiness while the responsible workflow establishes it.\\n- Exact assessed source commit plus source/instruction identities; complete file\\n  and workflow inventory, with omitted scope and reasons. For this repository's\\n  small package, inspect **all `ticket_harness` Python files**, including unchanged\\n  collaborators. Larger targets inventory the complete affected workflow and its\\n  collaborators. A list of files alone does not establish inspection.\\n- All prior unresolved full finding keys, original reports, disposition/decision/\\n  amendment/verification links, and pending exact-source revalidation. Record an\\n  explicit absence when there are none; retain optional/deferred observations.\\n\\nUse a **fresh High Standards assignment**, with the approved model, role guidance\\nand active-context target. Keep it distinct from routine ticket attempts and\\npreserve their independent Spec/Standards axes. Report unavailable settings.\\nArchitecture review guidance can inform the assessment; an HTML report is not\\nrequired for a checkpoint. The original report must explain actual owners,\\noperations, unchanged writers/consumers and restored/invalidation behavior. Trace\\nthe concrete edits for a plausible change (for example another review stage or\\nrepair-policy adjustment): which owner, callers, saved representation, restore,\\ninvalidation and checks would change, and why. Hashes and passing tests support\\nidentity/behavior, not the design conclusion.\\n\\nAn existing audit can satisfy this work only when its **exact source, complete\\nrequired coverage and retained dispositions** match the present checkpoint. Link\\nthat proof and the original audit. A changed source requires renewed relevant\\nreview; record scope and why any unchanged evidence remains applicable. Until\\nthat review names the current source, prior readiness is stale. Do not edit old\\nreports to make them appear current.\\n\\n## Findings and progression\\n\\nApply the [disposition and authority rules](review-records.md#dispositions-and-authority):\\nfixed needs independent verification of the exact repair revision; deferral needs\\na linked ticket and reason; consequential risk acceptance, blocker deferral,\\nchanged ownership or scope requires an actual human decision and applicable\\napproved amendment. Keep awaiting decisions and missing amendments unresolved.\\nPreserve original classification and append history; never obtain authority by\\nrelabeling a blocker. Optional suggestions remain optional and cause no automatic\\nrepair or approval request.\\n\\nRecord four separate observations, supported by source-specific evidence:\\n\\n| Observation | Meaning and next action |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Assessment completed | Required reports and inspection finished; findings can remain open. Missing reports mean in progress. |\\n| Findings resolved/dispositioned | Exact fixes independently verified or valid authority and linked follow-up retained. Open in-scope blockers still prevent affected integration/readiness. |\\n| Candidate ready for human QA | Required membership, current architecture work, finding gates and all existing exact-candidate checks are satisfied. Only the authorized QA workflow prepares the actual request. |\\n| Milestone accepted | Actual human QA decision accepts the exact candidate/inputs. Only this permits the subsequent milestone/promotion under the approved workflow. |\\n\\nA clean checkpoint progresses autonomously within approved scope; it supplies no\\nhuman acceptance. Authorized in-scope blockers use existing bounded repairs.\\nA cumulative blocker outside those bounds or a consequential undecided contract\\npreserves work and produces an incomplete human-decision handoff; independent\\nunaffected work may continue only under the existing eligibility rules. A review\\ndecision cannot resume a stopped run or accept a candidate.\\n\\n## Tracker and handoff\\n\\nLink the assessment from every originating ticket, the milestone and feature\\nreview index. Carry baseline/current source, complete scope, lifecycle, missing\\nreports, unresolved full keys, deferred ticket links, human decisions/amendments,\\nstale evidence and the next responsible action into implementation and QA/waiting\\nhandoffs. Distinguish an absent checkpoint from a clean one. Refresh the current\\nsummary while preserving reports and append-only history.\\n\\nPreserve approved active-run inputs and frozen QA snapshots. During a run, new\\nartifacts belong only in authorized harness output, with authoring links added\\nlater. If the requested authoring path is an active input, report that exact\\nblocker. Changed assessed source or frozen evidence invalidates relevant readiness;\\nnew proposals may remain outside frozen evidence pending renewed review/request.\\n\\nThe [ticket 18 walkthrough](../../.scratch/ticket-harness/evidence/18-walkthrough/README.md)\\nretains finite isolated observations. Its example human statements are synthetic;\\nits reviews do not assess or accept the real development milestone.\\n# Review report envelope: {{review ID}} / {{report ID}}\\n\\nTemplate: [record contract](../../agents/review-records.md). Preserve original\\nreviewer output verbatim at a unique evidence path; store this envelope alongside\\nit if the report lacks metadata. Record envelope/output digests in the assessment\\nor manifest after capture, outside the hashed file itself.\\n\\n| Field | Value |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Assessment | {{review ID and work item link}} |\\n| Report identity | {{report ID, axis, attempt, assignment and actor identity}} |\\n| Actual settings | {{model, reasoning, context target/observed setting or unavailable}} |\\n| Original baseline | {{full source identity}} |\\n| Assessed source | {{exact full revision or retained snapshot identity}} |\\n| Supplied instructions | {{paths, revision and digests/snapshot manifest; missing delivery evidence explicit}} |\\n| Original output/evidence | {{immutable relative links and capture manifest}} |\\n| Inspected scope | {{files/flows}} |\\n| Omitted scope | {{scope and reasons; unresolved assumptions}} |\\n| Prior observations | {{superseded/reopened full keys and links or none}} |\\n\\n## Substantive assessment\\n\\n{{Approved behavior/ownership contract; traced callers, writers, persistence/restore\\nand invalidation; reasoning about invariant preservation, including a justified\\nclean conclusion when appropriate. Apply the shared\\n[scope contract](../../agents/ownership-review.md#report-substantive-reasoning).}}\\n\\n## Evidence audit\\n\\n{{Commands/observations and identity checks, results and limits; distinguish these\\nfrom the substantive design conclusion.}}\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\nUse a heading per report-local ID. Preserve the reviewer's classification,\\nconcern, source location/evidence, consequence and proposed remedy. Dispositions\\nbelong in the separate append-only history, linked by the full compound key.\\n\\n### {{local ID}}\\n\\n{{Classification; concern; exact source/evidence; consequence; remedy; full key}}\\n\\n## Assessment result\\n\\n{{Assessment complete/incomplete; unresolved assumptions; original result.\\nThis snapshot is not a mutable current-disposition summary.}}\\n\"}"
        }
      ],
