        },
        {
          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "Warning: truncated output (original token count: 13970)\nTotal output lines: 1\n\n{\"chunk_id\":\"6f6a14\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000061857,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":13722,\"output\":\"---\\nname: codebase-design\\ndescription: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\\n---\\n\\n# Codebase Design\\n\\nDesign **deep modules**: a lot of behaviour behind a small interface, placed at a clean seam, testable through that interface. Use this language and these principles wherever code is being designed or restructured. The aim is leverage for callers, locality for maintainers, and testability for everyone.\\n\\n## Glossary\\n\\nUse these terms exactly: don't substitute \\\"component,\\\" \\\"service,\\\" \\\"API,\\\" or \\\"boundary.\\\" Consistent language is the whole point.\\n\\n**Module**: anything with an interface and an implementation. Deliberately scale-agnostic: a function, class, package, or tier-spanning slice. _Avoid_: unit, component, service.\\n\\n**Interface**: everything a caller must know to use the module correctly: the type signature, but also invariants, ordering constraints, error modes, required configuration, and performance characteristics. _Avoid_: API, signature (too narrow, they refer only to the type-level surface).\\n\\n**Implementation**: what's inside a module, its body of code. Distinct from **Adapter**: a thing can be a small adapter with a large implementation (a Postgres repo) or a large adapter with a small implementation (an in-memory fake). Reach for \\\"adapter\\\" when the seam is the topic; \\\"implementation\\\" otherwise.\\n\\n**Depth**: leverage at the interface. The amount of behaviour a caller (or test) can exercise per unit of interface they have to learn. A module is **deep** when a large amount of behaviour sits behind a small interface, **shallow** when the interface is nearly as complex as the implementation.\\n\\n**Seam** _(Michael Feathers)_: a place where you can alter behaviour without editing in that place; the *location* at which a module's interface lives. Where to put the seam is its own design decision, distinct from what goes behind it. _Avoid_: boundary (overloaded with DDD's bounded context).\\n\\n**Adapter**: a concrete thing that satisfies an interface at a seam. Describes *role* (what slot it fills), not substance (what's inside).\\n\\n**Leverage**: what callers get from depth. More capability per unit of interface they learn. One implementation pays back across N call sites and M tests.\\n\\n**Locality**: what maintainers get from depth. Change, bugs, knowledge, and verification concentrate in one place rather than spreading across callers. Fix once, fixed everywhere.\\n\\n## Deep vs shallow\\n\\n**Deep module** = small interface + lots of implementation:\\n\\n```\\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\\n\u2502   Small Interface   \u2502  \u2190 Few methods, simple params\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\\n\u2502                     \u2502\\n\u2502  Deep Implementation\u2502  \u2190 Complex logic hidden\\n\u2502                     \u2502\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\\n```\\n\\n**Shallow module** = large interface + little implementation (avoid):\\n\\n```\\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\\n\u2502       Large Interface           \u2502  \u2190 Many methods, complex params\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\\n\u2502  Thin Implementation            \u2502  \u2190 Just passes through\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\\n```\\n\\nWhen designing an interface, ask:\\n\\n- Can I reduce the number of methods?\\n- Can I simplify the parameters?\\n- Can I hide more complexity inside?\\n\\n## Principles\\n\\n- **Depth is a property of the interface, not the implementation.** A deep module can be internally composed of small, mockable, swappable parts; they just aren't part of the interface. A module can have **internal seams** (private to its implementation, used by its own tests) as well as the **external seam** at its interface.\\n- **The deletion test.** Imagine deleting the module. If complexity vanishes, it was a pass-through. If complexity reappears across N callers, it was earning its keep.\\n- **The interface is the test surface.** Callers and tests cross the same seam. If you want to test *past* the interface, the module is probably the wrong shape.\\n- **One adapter means a hypothetical seam. Two adapters means a real one.** Don't introduce a seam unless something actually varies across it.\\n\\n## Designing for testability\\n\\nGood interfaces make testing natural:\\n\\n1. **Accept dependencies, don't create them.**\\n\\n   ```typescript\\n   // Testable\\n   function processOrder(order, paymentGateway) {}\\n\\n   // Hard to test\\n   function processOrder(order) {\\n     const gateway = new StripeGateway();\\n   }\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Return results, don't produce side effects.**\\n\\n   ```typescript\\n   // Testable\\n   function calculateDiscount(cart): Discount {}\\n\\n   // Hard to test\\n   function applyDiscount(cart): void {\\n     cart.total -= discount;\\n   }\\n   ```\\n\\n3. **Small surface area.** Fewer methods = fewer tests needed. Fewer params = simpler test setup.\\n\\n## Relationships\\n\\n- A **Module** has exactly one **Interface** (the surface it presents to callers and tests).\\n- **Depth** is a property of a **Module**, measured against its **Interface**.\\n- A **Seam** is where a **Module**'s **Interface** lives.\\n- An **Adapter** sits at a **Seam** and satisfies the **Interface**.\\n- **Depth** produces **Leverage** for callers and **Locality** for maintainers.\\n\\n## Rejected framings\\n\\n- **Depth as ratio of implementation-lines to interface-lines** (Ousterhout): rewards padding the implementation. We use depth-as-leverage instead.\\n- **\\\"Interface\\\" as the TypeScript `interface` keyword or a class's public methods**: too narrow: interface here includes every fact a caller must know.\\n- **\\\"Boundary\\\"**: overloaded with DDD's bounded context. Say **seam** or **interface**.\\n\\n## Going deeper\\n\\n- **Deepening a cluster given its dependencies**, see [DEEPENING.md](DEEPENING.md): dependency categories, seam discipline, and replace-don't-layer testing.\\n- **Exploring alternative interfaces**, see [DESIGN-IT-TWICE.md](DESIGN-IT-TWICE.md): spin up parallel sub-agents to design the interface several radically different ways, then compare on depth, locality, and seam placement.\\n---\\nname: design-philosophy-object-oriented\\ndescription: Object-oriented design principles as a lens for system architecture \u2014 SOLID, composition over inheritance, the actor model, design patterns (and when they're wrong), encapsulation, polymorphism, and responsibility-driven design. Use when evaluating code organization, module boundaries, or object/component relationships.\\n---\\n\\n# Design Philosophy: Object-Oriented Design\\n\\nOO principles as thinking tools for code organization, module boundaries, and component relationships.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Core Premise\\n\\nObject-oriented design is not about classes and inheritance. It is about **managing the cost of change** by localizing decisions behind stable interfaces. Every OO principle exists to answer one question: *when a requirement changes, how many places in the code must change with it?*\\n\\nGood OO design means a single requirement change touches one or two files. Bad OO design means shotgun surgery across a class hierarchy that was supposed to make things \\\"extensible.\\\"\\n\\nThe principles below are thinking tools, not commandments. Each one has a failure mode that is just as common as the problem it solves. Know both.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. SOLID Principles\\n\\nSOLID is a diagnostic framework. When code is hard to change, one of these principles usually explains why. But applying them prophylactically \u2014 before you feel the pain \u2014 produces the kind of over-abstracted architecture that makes senior engineers weep.\\n\\n### S \u2014 Single Responsibility Principle\\n\\n**The principle:** A class should have one reason to change. \\\"Reason to change\\\" means *one stakeholder* or *one axis of requirements evolution*.\\n\\n**When it helps:**\\n- A class mixes persistence logic with business rules. The database schema changes, and you're editing the same file that implements pricing. Split them.\\n- A module handles both HTTP serialization and domain validation. These change for completely different reasons and at different rates.\\n\\n**When it hurts:**\\n- Taken too far, SRP produces dozens of anemic classes that each do one trivial thing, connected by a web of delegation that nobody can follow. You've traded one 200-line class for fifteen 20-line classes and a wiring problem.\\n- The question is never \\\"does this class do more than one thing?\\\" \u2014 everything does more than one thing at some level of abstraction. The question is: **do these responsibilities change at the same time, for the same reasons?** If yes, they belong together. Cohesion matters more than smallness.\\n\\n**The practical test:** Look at your git history. If a class changes in every commit for unrelated reasons, it has too many responsibilities. If you're always changing three classes together in lockstep, they may have been split incorrectly \u2014 they share a responsibility.\\n\\n### O \u2014 Open/Closed Principle\\n\\n**The principle:** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification. You should be able to add new behavior without editing existing, working code.\\n\\n**When it helps:**\\n- You have a payment system and need to add new payment methods regularly. A strategy pattern or plugin architecture lets you add `StripePayment`, `PayPalPayment` without touching the payment processing core.\\n- Framework extension points: middleware chains, hook systems, event handlers. These are OCP applied well.\\n\\n**When it hurts:**\\n- Premature openness. If you have one payment method and *might* add another someday, you don't need a `PaymentStrategy` interface with a factory and a registry. You need an `if` statement and the willingness to refactor later.\\n- Every extension point is a design commitment. It says \\\"I expect variation here.\\\" If you're wrong about where variation will occur, you've added complexity at the wrong seams and the actual change still requires modification.\\n\\n**The practical test:** You need OCP when you've added the third variant of something. The first is just code. The second might be an `if/else`. The third is when you extract the pattern. Not before.\\n\\n### L \u2014 Liskov Substitution Principle\\n\\n**The principle:** If `S` is a subtype of `T`, then objects of type `T` can be replaced with objects of type `S` without altering the correctness of the program. Subtypes must honor the behavioral contract of their parent, not just the type signature.\\n\\n**When it helps:**\\n- API design. If your function accepts a `Stream`, every subclass of `Stream` must behave like a stream \u2014 same ordering guarantees, same error semantics. Callers should never need to check `isinstance` to decide how to use it.\\n- Type hierarchies in domain models. An `Employee` subclassing `Person` works if every operation valid on a `Person` is valid on an `Employee`. It breaks the moment `Person` has a method that doesn't apply to all subtypes.\\n\\n**When it hurts:**\\n- The classic: `Square` inheriting from `Rectangle`. A square *is* a rectangle mathematically, but if `Rectangle` has independent `setWidth()` and `setHeight()`, `Square` cannot honor that contract without breaking its own invariant. The subtype relationship that seems obvious in the domain is *wrong* in code.\\n- This reveals the real lesson: **inheritance is a much stronger claim than most engineers realize.** It doesn't mean \\\"shares some properties.\\\" It means \\\"is fully substitutable in every context the parent appears.\\\" That's a hard contract to maintain as code evolves.\\n\\n**The practical test:** Can you delete the type check? If code does `if isinstance(x, SpecificSubclass)` to handle a special case, LSP is violated. The subtype is not substitutable. Either fix the subtype's behavior or admit the hierarchy is wrong.\\n\\n### I \u2014 Interface Segregation Principle\\n\\n**The principle:** Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. Prefer many small, role-specific interfaces over one large general-purpose interface.\\n\\n**When it helps:**\\n- A `Repository` interface with `find()`, `save()`, `delete()`, `bulkImport()`, `generateReport()`. Most clients only need `find()` and `save()`. The report generator only needs `find()`. Split into `Readable`, `Writable`, `BulkImportable` \u2014 each client depends on exactly what it uses.\\n- API surface area design. A module that exposes 40 public methods is hard to learn, hard to mock, and hard to evolve. Narrow interfaces let you change the implementation behind one role without affecting clients of another.\\n\\n**When it hurts:**\\n- Interface explosion. If you create a separate interface for every single method, you've traded one comprehensibility problem for another. Group by role: \\\"things a reader needs,\\\" \\\"things a writer needs.\\\" Not by method count.\\n- In dynamic languages, ISP often happens naturally through duck typing. You don't need a formal `Readable` interface in Python \u2014 just document which methods your function actually calls.\\n\\n**The practical test:** Look at your mocks in tests. If you're mocking 12 methods to test code that calls 2, your interface is too wide.\\n\\n### D \u2014 Dependency Inversion Principle\\n\\n**The principle:** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details.\\n\\n**When it helps:**\\n- Your business logic directly imports and calls `psycopg2`. Now you can't test it without a database, can't switch to a different store, and can't run it in a different context. Inverting the dependency \u2014 business logic defines a `Repository` interface, infrastructure provides the implementation \u2014 gives you testability and flexibility.\\n- Plugin systems. The host application defines interfaces; plugins implement them. The host never imports plugin code directly.\\n\\n**When it hurts:**\\n- **You do not need an interface for everything.** If there is exactly one implementation of `UserRepository` and there will only ever be one, the interface is ceremony. The test can use the real thing or a simple fake.\\n- Dependency injection frameworks that require you to wire 47 bindings in a configuration file before your application starts. The cure is worse than the disease. Constructor injection with sensible defaults covers 90% of cases.\\n- DI becomes pathological when every class takes 8 interfaces in its constructor and you need a dependency graph visualizer to understand how the application boots.\\n\\n**The practical test:** If adding a new feature requires editing a DI configuration file before writing any actual code, you've over-invested in inversion. If testing a class requires spinning up infrastructure, you've under-invested.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Composition Over Inheritance\\n\\n**The principle:** Favor object composition (has-a) over class inheritance (is-a) for code reuse and flexibility.\\n\\n### Why Inheritance Creates Brittleness\\n\\n- **The fragile base class problem.** A change to a base class method can silently break subclasses that override related methods, or that depend on the base class's internal call sequence. The subclass author made assumptions about superclass behavior that aren't part of any explicit contract.\\n- **Inheritance couples the subclass to the superclass's implementation**, not just its interface. If the superclass changes how `save()` internally calls `validate()`, every subclass that overrides `validate()` might break.\\n- **Inheritance hierarchies are hard to change** once established. Inserting a new level, changing the root class, or refactoring the hierarchy breaks all descendants. Composition relationships can be rewired individually.\\n- **Multiple inheritance** makes all of these problems worse by introducing ambiguity (the diamond problem) and making the call chain unpredictable.\\n\\n### Delegation as the Alternative\\n\\nInstead of `class EnhancedList extends List`, create `class EnhancedList` that *contains* a `List` and forwards the methods it wants to expose. This is more code upfront but:\\n- You control exactly which methods are exposed (no accidental API surface).\\n- You can swap the inner implementation without affecting clients.\\n- You can compose multiple behaviors without a hierarchy.\\n\\n### Mixins and Traits\\n\\nMixins (Python, Ruby) and traits (Rust, Scala) offer a middle path: reusable behavior chunks that don't create rigid hierarchies. They work well when:\\n- The behavior is genuinely orthogonal (serialization, logging, comparison).\\n- There's no shared mutable state between the mixin and the host class.\\n- The mixin doesn't depend on the host class's internal structure.\\n\\nThey go wrong when mixins start depending on each other's state, or when a class inherits from five mixins that all modify the same attributes.\\n\\n### When Inheritance IS Appropriate\\n\\nInheritance is the right tool when:\\n- There is a genuine **behavioral** is-a relationship with a stable contract. A `TcpSocket` is a `Socket`. An `HttpError` is an `Error`. The superclass defines a behavioral contract that changes rarely.\\n- You're implementing the **template method** pattern: the superclass defines the algorithm skeleton, subclasses fill in the steps. This works when the algorithm structure is stable and the variation points are well-defined.\\n- Framework extension points explicitly designed for inheritance (Django views, JUnit test cases). The framework author has done the work to make the base class inheritance-safe.\\n\\nInheritance is the **wrong** tool when:\\n- You just want to reuse some methods. Use composition.\\n- The \\\"is-a\\\" relationship is about data shape, not behavior. A `PremiumUser` having all the fields of a `User` plus some extras is a composition relationship, not an inheritance relationship.\\n- You find yourself overriding superclass methods to disable them (returning `NotImplemented`, throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`). This is a Liskov violation \u2014 the hierarchy is wrong.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. The Actor Model\\n\\n### Core Concepts\\n\\nThe actor model treats **actors** as the fundamental unit of computation. Each actor:\\n- Has private state that no other actor can access directly.\\n- Communicates exclusively through asynchronous **message passing**.\\n- Processes one message at a time (no internal concurrency).\\n- Can create new actors, send messages, and decide how to handle the next message.\\n\\nThis eliminates shared mutable state by construction. There are no locks, no mutexes, no race conditions on shared data \u2014 because there is no shared data.\\n\\n### Key Patterns\\n\\n**Mailboxes.** Each actor has a message queue (mailbox). Messages arrive asynchronously and are processed sequentially. This gives you concurrency between actors and sequential consistency within each actor.\\n\\n**Supervision trees** (Erlang/OTP, Akka). Actors are organized into hierarchies where parent actors supervise children. When a child fails, the supervisor decides: restart it, stop it, escalate. This turns \\\"what do we do when something crashes\\\" from an afterthought into an explicit architectural decision.\\n\\n**Location transparency.** An actor reference doesn't encode whether the actor is in the same process, on another machine, or in another data center. Message passing works the same way regardless. This makes distribution an operational concern, not a code change.\\n\\n**Behavior switching.** An actor can change its message-handling be\u20263970 tokens truncated\u2026ing to be a data structure. Keep them separate.\\n\\n### Data Flow Systems\\n\\nSome systems are fundamentally about data flowing through transformations: ETL pipelines, compilers, signal processing chains. The natural model is functional: inputs, transformations, outputs. Trying to model these as object interactions (the `Compiler` sends a message to the `Parser` which creates `ASTNodes` which are visited by `Optimizers`) usually produces worse code than a pipeline of pure functions.\\n\\n### Scripting and Glue Code\\n\\nA 50-line script that reads a file, transforms some data, and writes the output doesn't need a class hierarchy. It needs functions. Possibly just one function. The overhead of defining classes, instantiating objects, and managing their lifecycle is not justified for code that runs once and is read twice.\\n\\n### The Diagnostic\\n\\nAsk: **Is the system fundamentally about independent entities with behavior and state that interact with each other?** If yes, OO is likely a good fit. If the system is fundamentally about transforming data from one shape to another, or about coordinating a sequence of steps, other paradigms will serve better.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Applying the Philosophy\\n\\n### The Change Locality Test\\n\\nThe ultimate measure of OO design quality: **\\\"If I change this requirement, how many files do I touch?\\\"**\\n\\n- **Good OO:** \\\"We need to add a new discount type.\\\" You add one new class, register it in one place. Nothing else changes.\\n- **Bad OO:** \\\"We need to add a new discount type.\\\" You modify the `Discount` base class, update the `DiscountFactory`, add a case to `DiscountValidator`, modify `DiscountSerializer`, update `DiscountController`, and change three test files.\\n\\nIf adding a new variant means touching every layer of the system, the class hierarchy is providing organization without providing isolation. You've paid the complexity tax of OO without getting the benefit.\\n\\n### Refactoring Procedural Code Toward Good OO\\n\\n**Step 1: Identify clusters.** Look for groups of functions that operate on the same data. Functions that always take the same first argument are methods waiting to be born.\\n\\n**Step 2: Extract data classes.** Group related fields into objects. This is just data organization \u2014 no behavior yet.\\n\\n**Step 3: Move behavior to data.** For each function that operates on a data class, ask: should this be a method? Apply the Information Expert pattern \u2014 if the function needs the object's data to make a decision, the decision belongs on the object.\\n\\n**Step 4: Identify interfaces.** Where do you have switch statements or type-checking conditionals? Those are polymorphism waiting to happen. Extract an interface, create implementations.\\n\\n**Step 5: Stop.** Not everything needs to be a class. Leave utility functions as functions. Leave scripts as scripts. Leave data as data. The goal is not \\\"make everything OO.\\\" The goal is \\\"put decisions next to the data they depend on, and hide those decisions behind stable interfaces.\\\"\\n\\n### When to Stop Refactoring\\n\\nYou've gone far enough when:\\n- Each requirement change touches 1-3 files.\\n- You can explain what each class is *responsible for* in one sentence.\\n- You don't need to read the implementation to understand how to use the interface.\\n- Tests are easy to write without elaborate mocking.\\n\\nYou've gone too far when:\\n- You can't find where something happens without a dependency graph tool.\\n- Simple changes require modifying abstract base classes, interfaces, factories, and configuration.\\n- New team members need a week to understand the architecture.\\n- The abstraction layers outnumber the concrete implementations.\\n\\n### The Final Heuristic\\n\\nGood OO feels like the code already knows how to handle the change. You open the one file, make the one change, and everything else continues to work. Bad OO feels like you're negotiating with an architecture \u2014 convincing it to let you make a change it wasn't designed for, even though no design can anticipate every change. When the architecture is fighting you, it's wrong \u2014 regardless of how many patterns it uses.\\n---\\nname: test-strategy\\ndescription: Choose coverage and verification scope when adding or changing tests, or audit a slow, redundant, or flaky test suite. Use alongside TDD while preserving required checks and approved testing seams.\\n---\\n\\n# Test strategy\\n\\nManage the value and cost of regression coverage. [TDD](../../matt-pocock/tdd/SKILL.md) owns the red/green loop;\\nthis skill owns which behaviors need tests, which existing tests suffice, and\\nwhich checks to run. Read the repository's testing policy and required checks\\nbefore choosing verification scope. Follow applicable glossary and ADR guidance.\\n\\n## During implementation\\n\\nBefore adding tests, inspect the existing coverage at the approved seams. Make a\\nbrief map from each changed requirement or failure mode to an existing test, an\\nextension, or a missing case. Keep this map in the current task notes or completion\\nevidence; a separate document is usually unnecessary. Existing tests can satisfy\\na requirement without adding tests or manufacturing a failure in working code.\\n\\nFor each additional case, identify the plausible regression it would catch that\\nthe existing cases would miss. Expand combinations when they exercise different\\ndecisions or meaningful interactions. Equivalent inputs usually need representative\\ncases rather than every permutation. Preserve explicitly required scenarios and\\nregressions from demonstrated bugs; test counts and coverage percentages alone do\\nnot establish redundancy.\\n\\nChoose the least expensive approved public seam that can detect the actual\\nfailure, preserving behavior rather than internal structure. If the agreed seam\\nmakes necessary coverage impractical, surface the design tradeoff through the\\nproject's design workflow. A new seam requires the existing approval process;\\ninternal mocks are not a shortcut around it. Then use TDD for the missing behavior.\\n\\n## Verification and evidence\\n\\nChoose focused checks from the changed behavior and its callers, shared fixtures,\\nconfiguration, and dependencies. Broaden selection when impact is uncertain. Run\\nthe repository's required completion checks, including any required full-suite gate.\\nRerun checks when a failure, a relevant change, or a remaining uncertainty\\ninvalidates their evidence. Required exact-revision verification always takes\\nprecedence over reusing earlier results.\\n\\nPlan evidence collection before verification. Retain the required run's command,\\nexit result, tested source/test identity, logs, and necessary artifacts so the\\nsame run supports completion bookkeeping. If required evidence is missing, collect\\nit with a scoped additional check and explain the gap. Saving a second report\\ndoes not by itself justify replaying the entire scenario matrix. Refer to earlier\\nresults with their actual provenance; do not relabel them as tests of a new revision.\\n\\n## Suite audits\\n\\nAudit the wider suite when requested or when measured runtime, flakiness, or\\nmaintenance friction warrants it. Ordinary feature work needs only the local\\ncoverage assessment above. Scope an audit to the affected area or observed cost.\\n\\nUse existing timing/failure records first; collect missing timings during the\\nnext required run where possible. Group tests by behavior and failure mode,\\nincluding parameterized cases, and examine repeated setup and expensive processes.\\nFor a proposed deletion or consolidation, identify the surviving test and explain\\nwhich regression protections it retains. Verify the affected behavior and required\\ncompletion checks after changing tests. Share fixture builders where useful while\\npreserving scenario isolation. Keep uncertain coverage until it can be evaluated.\\n\\n## Existing implementation reviews\\n\\nGive the existing reviewers the coverage map, actual check evidence, and repository\\ntesting policy. Spec review checks required behavior and regressions. Standards\\nreview considers redundant coverage, unnecessary scenario combinations, costly\\nsetup, fixture ownership, isolation, and brittle assertions. A blocker needs a\\nconcrete correctness, reliability, or maintenance consequence; a preference alone\\nremains optional. This adds review guidance, not another reviewer or completion gate.\\n# Testing policy\\n\\nThis policy governs development of this repository's harness. The reusable\\n[test-strategy skill](../../.agents/skills/custom/test-strategy/SKILL.md) guides\\ncoverage selection and suite audits; [TDD](../../.agents/skills/matt-pocock/tdd/SKILL.md)\\nguides implementation. Read [Python environment guidance](python-environment.md)\\nbefore Python or checks. The [harness guide](../ticket-harness.md#python-and-checks)\\ndocuments the check commands and tool setup.\\n\\n## Coverage selection\\n\\nMap acceptance requirements to existing tests before adding cases. Extend or\\nreuse those tests where they already exercise the required behavior. For a new\\ncase, identify the distinct failure it detects. Keep the short map in the ticket's\\nexisting evidence or implementation report.\\n\\nThe approved harness seam is the public CLI with scripted external processes,\\nreal temporary Git repositories, and observable Git/report outcomes. Preserve that\\nagreement. A cheaper public module seam is a design proposal to resolve with the\\nhuman, not permission to replace behavioral coverage with internal mocks.\\n\\nCover meaningful role, attempt, and failure-policy differences and interactions.\\nUse representative equivalent inputs rather than automatically multiplying every\\ndimension. Explicit ticket scenarios and demonstrated regressions remain required.\\nSeveral acceptance criteria may share one scenario; separate test names do not\\nnecessarily imply separate coverage. Preserve isolated writable repositories even\\nwhen sharing fixture builders or setup helpers.\\n\\n## Which checks to run\\n\\n| Change or stage | Verification |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Development iteration | Focused tests for the behavior and affected callers/fixtures, plus relevant static checks. Expand when shared impact is uncertain. |\\n| Implementation ticket checkpoint | The full test suite and required strict type, lint, format, and whitespace checks once the final implementation is ready. Explicit ticket requirements also apply. |\\n| Further code, test, configuration, or dependency changes | Rerun affected checks and renew the full-suite completion result for the final implementation. |\\n| Packaging or entry-point changes | Include wheel/install and installed CLI checks. Include other installed scenarios when explicitly required or when they resolve a concrete uncertainty. |\\n| Standalone documentation or skill changes | Validate the changed documents/skills, links, routing, and relevant workflow decisions. Runtime tests are warranted when executable examples or runtime behavior are affected. |\\n| Bookkeeping or handoff after verified implementation | Check the changed records, links, and consistency. Preserve the implementation's recorded check provenance; prose-only bookkeeping does not require replaying its suite. |\\n\\nAn implementation ticket's full-suite gate remains required even when focused tests\\npass. This policy does not establish selective testing as a substitute for that\\ngate, or override a ticket's explicit checks. Missing or failing required checks\\nleave verification incomplete.\\n\\nFor focused runs, select existing files or cases by the behavior under change.\\nFor example, after reading the case to confirm its relevance:\\n\\n```bash\\npython -m pytest tests/test_repairs.py -q -k failed_check\\n```\\n\\nUse timing output during the required full run when investigating suite cost:\\n\\n```bash\\npython -m pytest -q --durations=20\\n```\\n\\nInspect prerequisite completion records and their relationship to the current\\nimplementation. Independently rerun a prerequisite scenario when there is a\\nspecific compatibility uncertainty; the final full suite already includes the\\nprerequisite regression tests. Fresh reviewers may reproduce selected behavior\\nwithout each repeating the full matrix.\\n\\n## Evidence and review\\n\\nArrange required evidence capture before running checks. Record the command,\\nexit result, actual source/test revision (and relevant uncommitted changes), and\\nretained logs/artifact locations. Existing test assertions and their results can\\nsupport several criteria. Preserve required CLI/Git evidence from that execution\\nwhere available; extra saved JSON is not automatically a reason for a second\\ncomplete fixture run. If a required observation was not retained, collect the\\nmissing evidence with a scoped run. Report remaining gaps rather than inferring\\nunobserved results.\\n\\nSupply the coverage map and this policy to the existing Spec and Standards\\nreviewers when tests change. Test value, unnecessary combinations, repeated setup,\\nfixture ownership, isolation, and reliability belong in the existing review.\\nOptional maintenance suggestions remain optional.\\n\\nFor a requested or measured suite audit, use the test-strategy skill. Propose\\nconsolidations with an explicit mapping to the surviving regression coverage and\\nbefore/after check evidence. Runtime and maintenance cost are useful measures;\\nreducing the test count alone is not a goal.\\n\\n## Harness verification remains separate\\n\\nThese choices concern how developers verify changes to the harness. During a\\nharness run, all configured baseline, lightweight, review, and exact-candidate\\ngates still apply. Source changes invalidate approvals under the existing rules.\\nA development test selection or a prior passing tree cannot authorize skipping\\na required gate or reusing approval for a different candidate revision.\\n# Ticket Execution\\n\\nThe workflow for carrying approved software work through implementation, verification, and local integration.\\n\\n## Language\\n\\n**Spec**:\\nThe agreed description of intended behavior, constraints, and acceptance requirements for a body of work.\\n_Avoid_: Plan (when referring to required outcomes)\\n\\n**Ticket**:\\nA bounded piece of work under an approved spec, with its own acceptance requirements and declared dependencies on other tickets.\\n\\n**Approved plan**:\\nThe human-accepted version of a spec, its tickets, milestone membership, and acceptance conditions.\\n\\n**Milestone**:\\nA planned group of ticket outcomes with human acceptance conditions that gates further feature work and promotion.\\n\\n**QA decision**:\\nThe human's recorded acceptance or request for corrections for a specific milestone candidate.\\n\\n**Skill selection**:\\nThe skills approved for a role's work, combining its required role skills, project defaults, and any applicable ticket additions.\\n\\n**Run**:\\nAn execution of the harness against an approved spec and selected tickets, together with its recorded outcomes.\\n\\n**Implementation attempt**:\\nA worker assignment to produce a ticket implementation and submit its result. Development and testing iterations within that assignment belong to the same attempt.\\n\\n**Repair attempt**:\\nAn implementation attempt made in response to a rejected submission, using the current changes and the reported failures as its starting point.\\n\\n**Blocking finding**:\\nA reviewer-reported issue that must be resolved before a ticket may be integrated. It can trigger repair without immediately putting the ticket into the blocked state.\\n\\n**Optional suggestion**:\\nA reviewer-reported improvement that does not prevent integration or require an automatic repair attempt.\\n\\n**Harness**:\\nThe system responsible for coordinating ticket execution, verification, bounded repairs, and integration for approved work.\\n_Avoid_: Coordinator agent, dispatcher agent\\n\\n**Target repository**:\\nThe Git repository in which the harness carries out approved work. Its project commands, standards, and constraints govern that work.\\n_Avoid_: Client repository\\n\\n**Integration branch**:\\nThe local destination branch into which verified changes are combined. Its role may be a feature branch or a development branch; integration does not imply permission to publish.\\n\\n**Feature branch**:\\nThe integration branch that accumulates verified ticket changes for one approved feature before milestone acceptance.\\n\\n**Development branch**:\\nThe shared integration branch that receives accepted milestone candidates from feature branches, conventionally named `dev`.\\n\\n**Promotion**:\\nThe advancement of the development branch to a verified milestone candidate accepted by the human.\\n\\n**Candidate merge**:\\nThe proposed combination of source changes and a destination branch, evaluated before that destination advances. A ticket candidate targets the feature branch; a milestone candidate targets the development branch.\\n\\n**Review work item**:\\nAn identified assessment of a defined source revision and scope, linked to its reports and outstanding findings. Completing the assessment does not itself resolve its findings or accept a milestone.\\n\\n**Review report**:\\nThe preserved result of one reviewer assignment against an identified source and scope, including inspected coverage, findings and unresolved assumptions.\\n\\n**Finding disposition**:\\nThe recorded treatment of a review finding, with its reason, authority and supporting evidence. Its history connects the original concern to a verified fix, linked deferral or explicit human decision.\\n\\n**Review decision**:\\nA human's recorded choice about consequential review risk, scope, ownership or deferral. It is separate from a QA decision accepting an exact milestone candidate.\\n\\n**Architecture checkpoint**:\\nA required cumulative assessment of responsibility and invariant ownership across an affected workflow before specified integration or milestone-readiness progression.\\n# Reusable harness with authority over local integration\\n\\nThe integration authority below is refined by [ADR-0002](0002-human-qa-gates-feature-promotion.md): automatic ticket integration targets a feature branch; milestone promotion into the development branch requires human QA acceptance.\\n\\nThis repository will own a reusable harness and shared role skills that can operate on other Git repositories. Each target repository supplies its project commands, standards, and constraints, so the execution workflow can be improved across projects while project-specific decisions stay with the project.\\n\\nOnce the human approves a spec and its tickets, the harness may implement tickets, run checks, request bounded repairs, and merge passing work into a local integration branch without further approval. Publishing changes remains a separate step; missing requirements, incompatible architectural choices, and exhausted repair attempts stop the affected ticket and surface a decision for the human. This permits unattended progress within approved work while preserving human ownership of scope and consequential design decisions.\\n# Human QA gates feature promotion\\n\\nAfter plan approval, the harness may automatically implement, verify, review, and integrate individual tickets into a feature branch, but promotion into the development branch requires human acceptance of an exact tested milestone candidate. This refines [ADR-0001](0001-reusable-harness-and-local-integration.md), separating continuous verified ticket integration from the human's acceptance of a meaningful increment instead of postponing all integration until the feature is finished.\\n\\nEach plan has a final QA milestone and may have earlier ones; the next milestone bounds ticket execution. QA decisions identify the approved plan version and candidate inputs, remain separate from immutable approved requirements, and become stale when those inputs change. Planned QA checkpoints permit deliberate stop and continuation; recovery from arbitrary interruptions remains deferred, as do hosted PR approval and publishing.\\n\"}"
        }
