          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "{\"chunk_id\":\"848a26\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.039373338,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1169,\"output\":\"     1\\t---\\n     2\\tname: test-strategy\\n     3\\tdescription: Choose coverage and verification scope when adding or changing tests, or audit a slow, redundant, or flaky test suite. Use alongside TDD while preserving required checks and approved testing seams.\\n     4\\t---\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\t# Test strategy\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\tManage the value and cost of regression coverage. [TDD](../../matt-pocock/tdd/SKILL.md) owns the red/green loop;\\n     9\\tthis skill owns which behaviors need tests, which existing tests suffice, and\\n    10\\twhich checks to run. Read the repository's testing policy and required checks\\n    11\\tbefore choosing verification scope. Follow applicable glossary and ADR guidance.\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\t## During implementation\\n    14\\t\\n    15\\tBefore adding tests, inspect the existing coverage at the approved seams. Make a\\n    16\\tbrief map from each changed requirement or failure mode to an existing test, an\\n    17\\textension, or a missing case. Keep this map in the current task notes or completion\\n    18\\tevidence; a separate document is usually unnecessary. Existing tests can satisfy\\n    19\\ta requirement without adding tests or manufacturing a failure in working code.\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\tFor each additional case, identify the plausible regression it would catch that\\n    22\\tthe existing cases would miss. Expand combinations when they exercise different\\n    23\\tdecisions or meaningful interactions. Equivalent inputs usually need representative\\n    24\\tcases rather than every permutation. Preserve explicitly required scenarios and\\n    25\\tregressions from demonstrated bugs; test counts and coverage percentages alone do\\n    26\\tnot establish redundancy.\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\tChoose the least expensive approved public seam that can detect the actual\\n    29\\tfailure, preserving behavior rather than internal structure. If the agreed seam\\n    30\\tmakes necessary coverage impractical, surface the design tradeoff through the\\n    31\\tproject's design workflow. A new seam requires the existing approval process;\\n    32\\tinternal mocks are not a shortcut around it. Then use TDD for the missing behavior.\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\t## Verification and evidence\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\tChoose focused checks from the changed behavior and its callers, shared fixtures,\\n    37\\tconfiguration, and dependencies. Broaden selection when impact is uncertain. Run\\n    38\\tthe repository's required completion checks, including any required full-suite gate.\\n    39\\tRerun checks when a failure, a relevant change, or a remaining uncertainty\\n    40\\tinvalidates their evidence. Required exact-revision verification always takes\\n    41\\tprecedence over reusing earlier results.\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\tPlan evidence collection before verification. Retain the required run's command,\\n    44\\texit result, tested source/test identity, logs, and necessary artifacts so the\\n    45\\tsame run supports completion bookkeeping. If required evidence is missing, collect\\n    46\\tit with a scoped additional check and explain the gap. Saving a second report\\n    47\\tdoes not by itself justify replaying the entire scenario matrix. Refer to earlier\\n    48\\tresults with their actual provenance; do not relabel them as tests of a new revision.\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t## Suite audits\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\tAudit the wider suite when requested or when measured runtime, flakiness, or\\n    53\\tmaintenance friction warrants it. Ordinary feature work needs only the local\\n    54\\tcoverage assessment above. Scope an audit to the affected area or observed cost.\\n    55\\t\\n    56\\tUse existing timing/failure records first; collect missing timings during the\\n    57\\tnext required run where possible. Group tests by behavior and failure mode,\\n    58\\tincluding parameterized cases, and examine repeated setup and expensive processes.\\n    59\\tFor a proposed deletion or consolidation, identify the surviving test and explain\\n    60\\twhich regression protections it retains. Verify the affected behavior and required\\n    61\\tcompletion checks after changing tests. Share fixture builders where useful while\\n    62\\tpreserving scenario isolation. Keep uncertain coverage until it can be evaluated.\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\t## Existing implementation reviews\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\tGive the existing reviewers the coverage map, actual check evidence, and repository\\n    67\\ttesting policy. Spec review checks required behavior and regressions. Standards\\n    68\\treview considers redundant coverage, unnecessary scenario combinations, costly\\n    69\\tsetup, fixture ownership, isolation, and brittle assertions. A blocker needs a\\n    70\\tconcrete correctness, reliability, or maintenance consequence; a preference alone\\n    71\\tremains optional. This adds review guidance, not another reviewer or completion gate.\\n\"}"
