          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "     1\t---\n     2\tname: design-philosophy-object-oriented\n     3\tdescription: Object-oriented design principles as a lens for system architecture \u2014 SOLID, composition over inheritance, the actor model, design patterns (and when they're wrong), encapsulation, polymorphism, and responsibility-driven design. Use when evaluating code organization, module boundaries, or object/component relationships.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Design Philosophy: Object-Oriented Design\n     7\t\n     8\tOO principles as thinking tools for code organization, module boundaries, and component relationships.\n     9\t\n    10\t---\n    11\t\n    12\t## Core Premise\n    13\t\n    14\tObject-oriented design is not about classes and inheritance. It is about **managing the cost of change** by localizing decisions behind stable interfaces. Every OO principle exists to answer one question: *when a requirement changes, how many places in the code must change with it?*\n    15\t\n    16\tGood OO design means a single requirement change touches one or two files. Bad OO design means shotgun surgery across a class hierarchy that was supposed to make things \"extensible.\"\n    17\t\n    18\tThe principles below are thinking tools, not commandments. Each one has a failure mode that is just as common as the problem it solves. Know both.\n    19\t\n    20\t---\n    21\t\n    22\t## 1. SOLID Principles\n    23\t\n    24\tSOLID is a diagnostic framework. When code is hard to change, one of these principles usually explains why. But applying them prophylactically \u2014 before you feel the pain \u2014 produces the kind of over-abstracted architecture that makes senior engineers weep.\n    25\t\n    26\t### S \u2014 Single Responsibility Principle\n    27\t\n    28\t**The principle:** A class should have one reason to change. \"Reason to change\" means *one stakeholder* or *one axis of requirements evolution*.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When it helps:**\n    31\t- A class mixes persistence logic with business rules. The database schema changes, and you're editing the same file that implements pricing. Split them.\n    32\t- A module handles both HTTP serialization and domain validation. These change for completely different reasons and at different rates.\n    33\t\n    34\t**When it hurts:**\n    35\t- Taken too far, SRP produces dozens of anemic classes that each do one trivial thing, connected by a web of delegation that nobody can follow. You've traded one 200-line class for fifteen 20-line classes and a wiring problem.\n    36\t- The question is never \"does this class do more than one thing?\" \u2014 everything does more than one thing at some level of abstraction. The question is: **do these responsibilities change at the same time, for the same reasons?** If yes, they belong together. Cohesion matters more than smallness.\n    37\t\n    38\t**The practical test:** Look at your git history. If a class changes in every commit for unrelated reasons, it has too many responsibilities. If you're always changing three classes together in lockstep, they may have been split incorrectly \u2014 they share a responsibility.\n    39\t\n    40\t### O \u2014 Open/Closed Principle\n    41\t\n    42\t**The principle:** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification. You should be able to add new behavior without editing existing, working code.\n    43\t\n    44\t**When it helps:**\n    45\t- You have a payment system and need to add new payment methods regularly. A strategy pattern or plugin architecture lets you add `StripePayment`, `PayPalPayment` without touching the payment processing core.\n    46\t- Framework extension points: middleware chains, hook systems, event handlers. These are OCP applied well.\n    47\t\n    48\t**When it hurts:**\n    49\t- Premature openness. If you have one payment method and *might* add another someday, you don't need a `PaymentStrategy` interface with a factory and a registry. You need an `if` statement and the willingness to refactor later.\n    50\t- Every extension point is a design commitment. It says \"I expect variation here.\" If you're wrong about where variation will occur, you've added complexity at the wrong seams and the actual change still requires modification.\n    51\t\n    52\t**The practical test:** You need OCP when you've added the third variant of something. The first is just code. The second might be an `if/else`. The third is when you extract the pattern. Not before.\n    53\t\n    54\t### L \u2014 Liskov Substitution Principle\n    55\t\n    56\t**The principle:** If `S` is a subtype of `T`, then objects of type `T` can be replaced with objects of type `S` without altering the correctness of the program. Subtypes must honor the behavioral contract of their parent, not just the type signature.\n    57\t\n    58\t**When it helps:**\n    59\t- API design. If your function accepts a `Stream`, every subclass of `Stream` must behave like a stream \u2014 same ordering guarantees, same error semantics. Callers should never need to check `isinstance` to decide how to use it.\n    60\t- Type hierarchies in domain models. An `Employee` subclassing `Person` works if every operation valid on a `Person` is valid on an `Employee`. It breaks the moment `Person` has a method that doesn't apply to all subtypes.\n    61\t\n    62\t**When it hurts:**\n    63\t- The classic: `Square` inheriting from `Rectangle`. A square *is* a rectangle mathematically, but if `Rectangle` has independent `setWidth()` and `setHeight()`, `Square` cannot honor that contract without breaking its own invariant. The subtype relationship that seems obvious in the domain is *wrong* in code.\n    64\t- This reveals the real lesson: **inheritance is a much stronger claim than most engineers realize.** It doesn't mean \"shares some properties.\" It means \"is fully substitutable in every context the parent appears.\" That's a hard contract to maintain as code evolves.\n    65\t\n    66\t**The practical test:** Can you delete the type check? If code does `if isinstance(x, SpecificSubclass)` to handle a special case, LSP is violated. The subtype is not substitutable. Either fix the subtype's behavior or admit the hierarchy is wrong.\n    67\t\n    68\t### I \u2014 Interface Segregation Principle\n    69\t\n    70\t**The principle:** Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. Prefer many small, role-specific interfaces over one large general-purpose interface.\n    71\t\n    72\t**When it helps:**\n    73\t- A `Repository` interface with `find()`, `save()`, `delete()`, `bulkImport()`, `generateReport()`. Most clients only need `find()` and `save()`. The report generator only needs `find()`. Split into `Readable`, `Writable`, `BulkImportable` \u2014 each client depends on exactly what it uses.\n    74\t- API surface area design. A module that exposes 40 public methods is hard to learn, hard to mock, and hard to evolve. Narrow interfaces let you change the implementation behind one role without affecting clients of another.\n    75\t\n    76\t**When it hurts:**\n    77\t- Interface explosion. If you create a separate interface for every single method, you've traded one comprehensibility problem for another. Group by role: \"things a reader needs,\" \"things a writer needs.\" Not by method count.\n    78\t- In dynamic languages, ISP often happens naturally through duck typing. You don't need a formal `Readable` interface in Python \u2014 just document which methods your function actually calls.\n    79\t\n    80\t**The practical test:** Look at your mocks in tests. If you're mocking 12 methods to test code that calls 2, your interface is too wide.\n    81\t\n    82\t### D \u2014 Dependency Inversion Principle\n    83\t\n    84\t**The principle:** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details.\n    85\t\n    86\t**When it helps:**\n    87\t- Your business logic directly imports and calls `psycopg2`. Now you can't test it without a database, can't switch to a different store, and can't run it in a different context. Inverting the dependency \u2014 business logic defines a `Repository` interface, infrastructure provides the implementation \u2014 gives you testability and flexibility.\n    88\t- Plugin systems. The host application defines interfaces; plugins implement them. The host never imports plugin code directly.\n    89\t\n    90\t**When it hurts:**\n    91\t- **You do not need an interface for everything.** If there is exactly one implementation of `UserRepository` and there will only ever be one, the interface is ceremony. The test can use the real thing or a simple fake.\n    92\t- Dependency injection frameworks that require you to wire 47 bindings in a configuration file before your application starts. The cure is worse than the disease. Constructor injection with sensible defaults covers 90% of cases.\n    93\t- DI becomes pathological when every class takes 8 interfaces in its constructor and you need a dependency graph visualizer to understand how the application boots.\n    94\t\n    95\t**The practical test:** If adding a new feature requires editing a DI configuration file before writing any actual code, you've over-invested in inversion. If testing a class requires spinning up infrastructure, you've under-invested.\n    96\t\n    97\t---\n    98\t\n    99\t## 2. Composition Over Inheritance\n   100\t\n   101\t**The principle:** Favor object composition (has-a) over class inheritance (is-a) for code reuse and flexibility.\n   102\t\n   103\t### Why Inheritance Creates Brittleness\n   104\t\n   105\t- **The fragile base class problem.** A change to a base class method can silently break subclasses that override related methods, or that depend on the base class's internal call sequence. The subclass author made assumptions about superclass behavior that aren't part of any explicit contract.\n   106\t- **Inheritance couples the subclass to the superclass's implementation**, not just its interface. If the superclass changes how `save()` internally calls `validate()`, every subclass that overrides `validate()` might break.\n   107\t- **Inheritance hierarchies are hard to change** once established. Inserting a new level, changing the root class, or refactoring the hierarchy breaks all descendants. Composition relationships can be rewired individually.\n   108\t- **Multiple inheritance** makes all of these problems worse by introducing ambiguity (the diamond problem) and making the call chain unpredictable.\n   109\t\n   110\t### Delegation as the Alternative\n   111\t\n   112\tInstead of `class EnhancedList extends List`, create `class EnhancedList` that *contains* a `List` and forwards the methods it wants to expose. This is more code upfront but:\n   113\t- You control exactly which methods are exposed (no accidental API surface).\n   114\t- You can swap the inner implementation without affecting clients.\n   115\t- You can compose multiple behaviors without a hierarchy.\n   116\t\n   117\t### Mixins and Traits\n   118\t\n   119\tMixins (Python, Ruby) and traits (Rust, Scala) offer a middle path: reusable behavior chunks that don't create rigid hierarchies. They work well when:\n   120\t- The behavior is genuinely orthogonal (serialization, logging, comparison).\n   121\t- There's no shared mutable state between the mixin and the host class.\n   122\t- The mixin doesn't depend on the host class's internal structure.\n   123\t\n   124\tThey go wrong when mixins start depending on each other's state, or when a class inherits from five mixins that all modify the same attributes.\n   125\t\n   126\t### When Inheritance IS Appropriate\n   127\t\n   128\tInheritance is the right tool when:\n   129\t- There is a genuine **behavioral** is-a relationship with a stable contract. A `TcpSocket` is a `Socket`. An `HttpError` is an `Error`. The superclass defines a behavioral contract that changes rarely.\n   130\t- You're implementing the **template method** pattern: the superclass defines the algorithm skeleton, subclasses fill in the steps. This works when the algorithm structure is stable and the variation points are well-defined.\n   131\t- Framework extension points explicitly designed for inheritance (Django views, JUnit test cases). The framework author has done the work to make the base class inheritance-safe.\n   132\t\n   133\tInheritance is the **wrong** tool when:\n   134\t- You just want to reuse some methods. Use composition.\n   135\t- The \"is-a\" relationship is about data shape, not behavior. A `PremiumUser` having all the fields of a `User` plus some extras is a composition relationship, not an inheritance relationship.\n   136\t- You find yourself overriding superclass methods to disable them (returning `NotImplemented`, throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`). This is a Liskov violation \u2014 the hierarchy is wrong.\n   137\t\n   138\t---\n   139\t\n   140\t## 3. The Actor Model\n   141\t\n   142\t### Core Concepts\n   143\t\n   144\tThe actor model treats **actors** as the fundamental unit of computation. Each actor:\n   145\t- Has private state that no other actor can access directly.\n   146\t- Communicates exclusively through asynchronous **message passing**.\n   147\t- Processes one message at a time (no internal concurrency).\n   148\t- Can create new actors, send messages, and decide how to handle the next message.\n   149\t\n   150\tThis eliminates shared mutable state by construction. There are no locks, no mutexes, no race conditions on shared data \u2014 because there is no shared data.\n   151\t\n   152\t### Key Patterns\n   153\t\n   154\t**Mailboxes.** Each actor has a message queue (mailbox). Messages arrive asynchronously and are processed sequentially. This gives you concurrency between actors and sequential consistency within each actor.\n   155\t\n   156\t**Supervision trees** (Erlang/OTP, Akka). Actors are organized into hierarchies where parent actors supervise children. When a child fails, the supervisor decides: restart it, stop it, escalate. This turns \"what do we do when something crashes\" from an afterthought into an explicit architectural decision.\n   157\t\n   158\t**Location transparency.** An actor reference doesn't encode whether the actor is in the same process, on another machine, or in another data center. Message passing works the same way regardless. This makes distribution an operational concern, not a code change.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Behavior switching.** An actor can change its message-handling behavior in response to messages. This is a natural fit for state machines: an actor in the `authenticating` state handles messages differently than one in the `active` state.\n   161\t\n   162\t### When Actors Are Right\n   163\t\n   164\t- **Concurrent, stateful, isolated units.** A chat room that manages its participant list. A device controller that tracks hardware state. A shopping cart that accumulates items. Each of these is naturally an actor: private state, message-driven interaction, independent lifecycle.\n   165\t- **Systems that need fault isolation.** One actor crashing should not bring down the system. Supervision trees make this explicit.\n   166\t- **Distributed systems** where you need location transparency and message-based communication anyway.\n   167\t\n   168\t### When Actors Are Overkill\n   169\t\n   170\t- **Stateless request processing.** If your web handler reads a request, queries a database, and returns a response with no in-memory state between requests, actors add complexity with no benefit. Use a thread pool or async handlers.\n   171\t- **Tight computational coupling.** If two components need to share a data structure and operate on it in tight loops, message-passing overhead is unacceptable. Use shared memory with proper synchronization.\n   172\t- **Simple pub/sub.** If you just need \"when X happens, notify Y,\" an event bus or observer pattern is simpler than a full actor system.\n   173\t- **When you don't have the infrastructure.** Actor systems need good tooling for debugging, tracing, and monitoring. Without it, you're debugging distributed state machines with print statements.\n   174\t\n   175\t---\n   176\t\n   177\t## 4. Design Patterns \u2014 When They're Right and Wrong\n   178\t\n   179\tThe Gang of Four book is a **diagnostic tool**, not a cookbook. Each pattern is a named solution to a recurring design problem. If you don't have the problem, you don't need the pattern. Cargo-culting patterns because they sound sophisticated produces code that is harder to read, harder to debug, and harder to change \u2014 the exact opposite of what patterns are for.\n   180\t\n   181\t### Creational Patterns\n   182\t\n   183\t**Factory Method / Abstract Factory**\n   184\t\n   185\t*The problem it solves:* The calling code shouldn't know which concrete class to instantiate. The decision depends on configuration, environment, or runtime state.\n   186\t\n   187\t*The problem it creates:* Indirection. You're reading code that calls `createWidget()` and you have to chase through a factory hierarchy to find out what actually gets created. If there's only one implementation, the factory is a pointless layer.\n   188\t\n   189\t*The simpler alternative:* A constructor. If you have one implementation, use `new Thing()` or just call the class directly. Extract a factory when you have the second implementation, not before.\n   190\t\n   191\t*The signal you need it:* You're writing `if config == \"postgres\": return PostgresRepo() elif config == \"mysql\": return MysqlRepo()` in three different places. That's when a factory earns its keep.\n   192\t\n   193\t**Builder**\n   194\t\n   195\t*The problem it solves:* Constructing complex objects with many optional parameters. A constructor with 12 parameters is unreadable and error-prone.\n   196\t\n   197\t*The problem it creates:* Builders are verbose. In languages with named parameters and default values (Python, Kotlin), a builder is often unnecessary ceremony.\n   198\t\n   199\t*The simpler alternative:* Named parameters with defaults. A configuration dataclass. A builder is most valuable in languages without named parameters (Java, C++) or when construction requires multi-step validation.\n   200\t\n   201\t**Singleton**\n   202\t\n   203\t*The problem it solves:* Ensuring exactly one instance of a class exists and providing global access to it.\n   204\t\n   205\t*The problem it creates:* Global state. Hidden dependencies. Testing nightmares (every test shares the same instance). Concurrency hazards. Singletons are the #1 pattern that engineers reach for and later regret.\n   206\t\n   207\t*The simpler alternative:* Create one instance and pass it around. Dependency injection. Module-level instances (in Python, a module is already a singleton). If you truly need \"only one,\" make that a deployment concern, not a code constraint.\n   208\t\n   209\t*The rare case it's justified:* Hardware resources that genuinely have a single physical instance (the display, the GPU context). Even then, consider whether you can pass the reference explicitly.\n   210\t\n   211\t### Structural Patterns\n   212\t\n   213\t**Adapter**\n   214\t\n   215\t*The problem it solves:* Making an existing class work with an interface it wasn't designed for. You have a `ThirdPartyLogger` but your code expects a `Logger` interface.\n   216\t\n   217\t*The problem it creates:* An extra class that does almost nothing. If you own both sides, just change one of them.\n   218\t\n   219\t*When it's right:* You don't own the adapted class (third-party library), or changing it would break other consumers. Adapters are legitimate anti-corruption layers.\n   220\t\n   221\t**Facade**\n   222\t\n   223\t*The problem it solves:* A complex subsystem with many interacting classes presents a simplified interface to the rest of the system.\n   224\t\n   225\t*The problem it creates:* The facade can become a god object that everyone depends on. It can also become a leaky abstraction when callers need access to subsystem details the facade doesn't expose.\n   226\t\n   227\t*When it's right:* Almost always. Facades are the most consistently useful structural pattern. Every well-designed module boundary is a facade. The key: make sure the facade represents a genuine abstraction, not just \"all the methods of the subsystem dumped into one class.\"\n   228\t\n   229\t**Decorator**\n   230\t\n"
        }
